src/backend/access/prolly/README

Prolly storage
==============

This directory holds version control for Postgres: tables that can be
branched, committed, diffed, merged, cloned, pushed and pulled.

A database's versioned schemas (CREATE SCHEMA ... VERSIONED) are the part
under version control.  Their tables use the table access method "prolly"
(prolly_table.c): heap's pages, read and written by heap's code, kept in
block-mapped storage (storage/smgr/bmap.c), so each branch's copy of a
table shares its pages copy-on-write with the others.  That working set is
ordinary Postgres: MVCC, WAL, indexes, constraints and triggers all work
as they do on heap.  One lock-order rule comes with it: never wait for a
buffer's content lock while holding a relation's block map header (or
another of its map pages).  Preparing a page for a change, and asking
whether it is shared, read the map with the page locked; a share or a
snapshot that must read the relation's pages under its header tries their
locks, and lets go of the header to wait.

A commit folds the session branch's versioned schemas into canonical,
content-addressed prolly trees, kept in the database's chunk store, and
names the canonical root (canon_folded.c).  Diffs and merges compare
those trees; remotes exchange their chunks.

Each versioned object has catalog rows on every branch that has used it,
each carrying its branch (catalog/branch.c).  Every branch-aware catalog
has an index on its branch column alone (pg_shdepend one on its database
and branch), and what reads a branch's objects reads them by it
(catalog_branch_beginscan()): a branch's first use copies the rows on its
source, a branch's delete removes the rows on it, and a commit's
canonical form, and the checks of a working set against a commit, read
the rows on the session's branch.  That work is in the objects on the
branch, however many rows other branches and shared objects have.  One
catalog is still read whole for the canonical form: pg_cast
(describe_casts()), since a cast between a type an extension has in a
versioned schema and a type outside it, made outside the extension, is
shared yet described; pg_cast grows only with user casts.

A session switching branch (set_session_branch()) drops the caches that
may hold the other branch's rows: every catalog cache entry and list of a
branch-aware catalog, and every relation cache entry but system catalogs'
(InvalidateBranchCaches()).  The caches of catalogs with no branch column
(pg_authid, pg_database, pg_am and the like), which every branch reads the
same, and the system catalogs' own relation entries, built only from rows
of objects in pg_catalog, are kept, so the statements after a checkout
don't rebuild them.


Interfaces and implementations
------------------------------

The current engine uses the following interfaces and registered components.
These describe the existing code; there is no plan to build alternative engines.
Changes to this design are tracked in GitHub issues:

	Interface         Header                  Implementations
	chunker           prolly_chunker.h        bytes, stub
	content_hash      prolly_hash.h           blake3, sha256, stub
	key_encoder       prolly_keyenc.h         ordered, stub
	value_encoder     prolly_valenc.h         canonical, stub
	reclamation       prolly_reclaim.h        copying, stub
	refs              prolly_refs.h           copies, stub
	transfer          prolly_transfer.h       chunks, stub
	diff_merge        prolly_merge.h          folded, stub
	tree              prolly_tree.h           cow, stub
	table_keying      prolly_keying.h         pk_or_prefix, stub
	canonicalizer     prolly_canon.h          folded, stub
	chunk_store       prolly_chunkstore.h     journal
	chunk_format      prolly_chunkformat.h    basic
	row_applier       prolly_applier.h        replication, stub
	table_file        prolly_tablefile.h      basic, stub
	change_finder     prolly_changes.h        pages, log, stub
	change_log        prolly_changelog.h      relation, stub
	remote            prolly_remote.h         file
	value_memo        prolly_memo.h           shared, stub
	writers           prolly_writers.h        modes, stub
	move_planner      prolly_moveplan.h       costs, stub
	matcache          prolly_matcache.h       branches, stub
	json_tree         prolly_jsontree.h       paths, stub
	node_cache        prolly_nodecache.h      shared, stub
	commit_cache      prolly_commitcache.h    shared, stub
	history_window    prolly_window.h         archive, stub

An interface header declares a routine struct: a ProllyImplInfo (interface,
name, impl_id, format_version) followed only by callback pointers, with the
contract written as comments.  An implementation is one or more source files
here, named <interface>_<implementation>.c, that define a const routine
struct.  Nothing else includes an implementation's private headers or calls
its functions.

Callers go through PROLLY_CALL(routine, callback, args...) (prolly_impl.h).
A NULL callback means "not implemented", and PROLLY_CALL raises
ERRCODE_FEATURE_NOT_SUPPORTED naming the interface, implementation and
callback.  So a stub is a routine with every callback NULL, and a real
implementation can be filled in one callback at a time.

Built-in implementations are listed in src/include/access/prolly_impllist.h,
the one place that names them, and found by interface and name through the
registry (prolly_registry.c).  Libraries in shared_preload_libraries can
register more with prolly_register_impl().  Interfaces keep their numbers,
so the enum has gaps where interfaces were removed;
prolly_interface_valid() says which numbers are interfaces.

Which implementation is used comes from configuration (prolly_config.c),
never from code: a prolly table's history is folded with its access
method's profile (keying, encoders, hash, chunker, trees), and a
database's version control is its profile (refs, transfer, diff and
merge, canonicalizer).


Canonical values
----------------

Rows and keys are folded in canonical form: the same bytes on every
platform, in every cluster and session, whatever the table's DDL history,
with no local OIDs.  prolly_datum.c holds the canonical encoding of single
values, shared by the key and value encoders as a fixed part of the format:
send formats produced with the client encoding fixed to UTF-8
(SetClientEncodingOverride in mbutils.c), and OID-free encodings of arrays
and composites.  valenc_canonical.c stores live columns only, without
trailing NULLs, and decodes rows encoded under an earlier layout.

A reader that needs only some columns asks for those (the value encoder's
decode_columns(), the keying's decode_row_columns()): the others are
skipped by their length prefixes, so no type decoder runs for them and no
long value of theirs is read from its tree.  fields() gives each column's
encoded bytes, or its value tree's reference, without decoding anything,
for callers that compare columns by their bytes (two rows hold a column
alike exactly when its bytes are the same) and decode only some
(decode_field()).  pg_stat_prolly counts the columns decoded
(columns_decoded) and the long values read whole (values_read,
value_bytes_read).

Folded format 17 (key/value encoder version 6) normalizes binary I/O for
extension base types and for ranges whose bounds reach one (including
through a composite or array subtype): send(receive(send(value))).
Arrays, composites and domains normalize their children recursively.  The
fixed built-in codecs keep their existing bytes and avoid this extra work.
This makes a value's stored bytes match its reconstructed representation:
for example, PostGIS can read either signed-NaN POINT spelling as POINT EMPTY.
As with binary dump/restore, reconstruction preserves the receiver's value;
it does not promise to preserve a physical spelling that receive discards.
Working-side cell merges fold and decode values before comparison, so the
same normalization applies there.

A normalized primary key must compare equal to its original value under
the actual key operator class.  Encoding rejects values that cross that
boundary, including during writes and adding a key to existing rows.  This
keeps the tree's key compatible with native index lookups into the heap.
Reconstruction also re-inserts rows through the table's other unique and
exclusion indexes, so a value such an index constrains is held to the same
rule: equal under a unique btree key column's operator class, and otherwise
(an exclusion operator, an index expression or predicate) unchanged by the
round trip.  Writes and building such an index over existing rows refuse
values that would not reconstruct.  Other non-key values may normalize
without that restriction.  Extension I/O must
still be stable after reconstruction; testing one value is not a certificate
that an arbitrary extension's send/receive functions satisfy that contract.

An extension's receive may be stricter than its input: PostGIS's text input
takes a one-point LINESTRING or an unclosed ring that its receive refuses.
Such a value, which a table may already hold, is kept as its text form
(tag 1) instead.  Only a data exception (class 22), or an internal error
raised by the type's own code rather than the server's (by dladdr(): an
error from a static or inlined server function is taken as the type's,
and on Windows every internal error is), counts as a refusal; anything else (a cancel, a deadlock, a lock not available, a
catalog lookup failing) is raised, so a value has one form fold after fold
and whatever it is folded with.  The text must read back as a value the type
sends as it sends this one, and the value is stored as its text reads
back: a geometry with a cached bounding box reads back without the cache,
which its send form doesn't hold.  As
receive is the extension's code, a refusal is caught in a subtransaction:
a fold encodes each table's rows as one batch (prolly_datum_batch()), with
receive unprotected inside one subtransaction, and a refusal rolls the
batch back and runs it again with a subtransaction per value.  Encoding
outside a batch takes one per value.  Not caught: in a parallel worker (a
parallel index build), where no subtransaction can be begun; while the
caller holds off interrupts (a buffer's content lock, a critical
section), so encoders read a page's copy, not the page; and for a range
over a row type holding such a type, whose values are untagged;
there a refusal is raised as before.  Text forms are written and read
with extra_float_digits, DateStyle, IntervalStyle, bytea_output (hex),
TimeZone (UTC), timezone_abbreviations (Default) and lc_monetary, lc_numeric
and lc_time (C) fixed, so a value's canonical form doesn't depend on the
committer's session (since folded format 20).  Fixing them costs a GUC nest
level and a setting changed for each that differs, so loops over many
values fix them once (prolly_datum_fixed_text()): a fold's batch, a change
finder's walk of a table (a status, a diff), the rows a reset or merge
writes (as a restore runs under the settings its dump fixes), and a row's
values checked as it is written.  Definitions are described under the same
settings, with search_path pg_catalog and quote_all_identifiers off
(prolly_datum_fix_text_settings(), at the describer's own GUC nest level),
so a constant in a default, a CHECK, a generated column, an index's
expression, a view or a policy prints alike in any session, and a move
runs the descriptions it makes the definitions again from under them too
(since folded format 21).  A merge (a cherry-pick, a revert) converts the
values it reads across a column's change of type, by the column's cast or
as the value's text form, under the same fixed settings, and lists its
conflicts so too: the same merge makes the same rows and the same commit
in any session.  The statements a merge replays to make the branch's
definitions the merged ones (an ALTER TABLE ... TYPE's USING, an added
column's default, the CHECKs the rows are tested against) run under them
as well, as do the refreshes of the materialized views it merges, so a
merge succeeds or fails, and makes its rows, alike in any session.  A
side's own ALTER TABLE ... TYPE converts under that session's settings,
so where a side's ALTER ran under others, a value it wrote can differ from
the one the merge makes of base's (a timestamptz made text under TimeZone
Asia/Tokyo, which the merge reads in UTC).


Extension ordering and history compatibility
-------------------------------------------

Folded formats since 16 record the extensions ordering each table's primary key,
including comparators reached through arrays, composites, ranges, multiranges
and domains.  A dependency's optional flags have one order: "versioned", then
"ordering".  Clone and move targets require the exact recorded ordering
versions; ordinary dependencies continue to require a compatible minimum.
A move checks the target, not the old head that it replaces.

Historical reads and merges check ordering per table.  After an extension
upgrade, a table whose definition and row types still match can be read by
re-sorting its encoded keys under the installed comparator.  This spills at
maintenance_work_mem and preserves row/value bytes and long-value references;
no commit is rewritten.  A concurrent definition or row-type change is
refused for that table.  Unrelated history remains readable.  Upgrades must
still preserve the ability to decode the stored values; there is no general
extension binary-compatibility certificate.  Re-sort results are not cached.

An upgrade invalidates baseline ordering and the row-only autocommit shortcut,
including a legacy head missing ordering flags.  Rebuild native indexes when
the extension's comparator changes, then commit to record its new ordering.
Hard reset to a target recording the old version remains refused (including
HEAD); a forward reset to a compatible new target is allowed.

This is a deliberate history format change.  Earlier servers cannot read
format-17 commits, so these commits must not be
sent to an older server or used after downgrading.  Current code still parses
legacy dependency entries, but it does not retroactively certify legacy key
ordering.  Older root formats retain the existing recommit requirement;
committing a branch writes its current working state in the new format.


Extension configuration rows
----------------------------

An extension's configuration tables (pg_extension.extconfig, such as
PostGIS's spatial_ref_sys) are shared by every branch and aren't
versioned, but the commits' data may refer to their rows (a column of a
custom SRID).  So since folded format 19 an extension's dependency
records, after its flags, what pg_dump dumps with it (prolly_extconfig.c):
each row its extcondition selects (a user's, not its script's), column by
column by name, each value in its canonical form, so a row is recorded the
same whatever the committer's settings; and the state of each
configuration sequence, and of each sequence a configuration table owns
(an identity's, a serial's), once it has moved from its start.  They are
read as the tables' owners with row security off, a search path of
pg_catalog alone and fixed text settings, as pg_dump reads them, so
nothing in the committer's schemas runs as the owner.  An extension
without such rows is recorded as before.  A clone writes the rows its
configuration tables lack into the extension it makes (by column name,
overriding identities, generated columns left to be generated, through
one prepared INSERT per table) and sets its sequences, as a restore of a
dump does.  A move (checkout, reset, merge, cherry-pick, revert) to
commits recording a row the database lacks is refused, naming the table;
rows the database has besides are its own.  A table with a primary key
(or a replica identity index) has its rows matched by key, so a row whose
other columns were edited since is still there; heads recording one key
with different values give a clone the checked-out branch's row, with a
warning naming the table and the key.  A clone's writes check its
extension's domains (on a column, in an array or a row) and lengths, as a
restore's input would.  A keyless table's
rows are matched whole, each as many times as recorded.  An element
naming anything but one of the extension's configuration tables (or a
sequence one owns) is refused, nothing an element holds is put in a query
as text, and decoded text is checked to be valid in the database's
encoding, as text's receive checks it.  The dependency tree is taken from
the backend's cache only while a digest of the configuration tables
matches: each visible row's place and inserting transaction, read without
encoding any row.


Identity across renames
-----------------------

A table or column keeps its identity when it is renamed: the name it was
made with, and which of the objects made with that name it is (0 unless
another holding that identity was there, as a renamed column is when a
new one takes its old name).  A root holds no identities, so the same
tables, columns and rows give the same root however they were named
before.  A commit records its root's identities beside it (its "identity"
item): only those that differ from the object's own name with 0, so a
commit whose objects were never renamed records nothing.  Commit ids already
depend on history, through their parents and metadata; two commits with
the same root can still record different identities.

A branch's working set finds its identities through its own relations
and attribute numbers, which a rename keeps and a drop and an add don't.
The refs keep an anchor for each branch ('I'), written by each commit and
after each move.  The anchor is valid only for the head it was made at.
Each live table and column is found in the anchor; one the anchor hasn't
is new since and gets its own name's identity (the first of that name's
that no live object holds).  Without an anchor of the head (the branch
moved without one made), each is found by its name in the head's identity
record.  So a column dropped and another of its name added gets the
dropped column's identity if that was its own name's, as Dolt's tags do,
but never the identity of a column renamed into the name.  Identities are
read from the catalogs, without opening (or locking) any relation, and a
read of one table identifies that table alone.

Reads match versions by identity.  dolt_at, dolt_history_table,
dolt_diff_table, row diffs, and a merge's conflict listing read each
version's columns under the names the working set gives the same columns.
Diffs read its tables that way too, and a summary (the canonicalizer's
changes()) pairs a table renamed as one renamed table, its rows and
definition perhaps changed too.  dolt_at and dolt_history_table read a
version's table by its name, as Dolt's AS OF and history do.  Tables
alike but unrelated are never paired.  The canonicalizer's renames()
works out the renames and rename_description() applies them in memory,
so nothing is written.  A merge names base, ours and theirs as one, using
merge_renames().  An
object renamed on one side takes that side's name.  Renamed differently
on both sides, or two kept objects given one name, is a schema conflict.
The branch's own tables and columns are renamed to the merged names first
(rename_objects(), ALTER TABLE ... RENAME), so their rows stay where they
are and only the other side's changes are written.  The anchor is then the
merge's.

A history read of a table's rows (dolt_at, dolt_history_table, dolt_diff,
dolt_diff_table, by row type or by name) takes SELECT on the table here
of the version's name, and that grant covers only the versions that are
that table, as relation-made history: every commit this database makes of
a branch records the relations its working set's tables were (refs
format 18: in a tree of records apart from the refs, under each
commit's id, its root the refs' 'F' entry, so that the refs a commit
changes stay as small, and a commit's work as flat, however many commits
came before it, and the entries a commit changes are applied to the refs
in one edit as it publishes; each table's, partitioned table's and
materialized view's
OID, owner's OID, kind, owner's name and qualified name, a line each,
the names'
backslashes and newlines escaped so that no name makes a line of its
own, and since format 16 quoted as with quote_all_identifiers off in any
session, as commits name tables; read strictly, a damaged record
refused; a record isn't a reference a collection follows, so after each
collection, as a step of its own, the records of the commits the store
no longer has are forgotten, with the shared entries of routines and
types ('J') no record left names: refs' forget_records(); a transaction
whose commit found such an entry stored, and so didn't write it, puts it
back when it publishes over a root moved since), and a version is the
table's when its commit was made from the table's relation, reached back
from the working set through the head and each commit's parents, a step
taken only where both commits were made from it (its name at each, as
the record has it, so a rename is followed), or forward from one of them
along another branch's commits made from it.  A version of another table
of its name, one dropped before this one was made, is read only by the
database's owner or a member of pg_read_all_data, as is a commit without
a record: one made in another database (fetched, pulled, cloned or pushed
here).  Commits name tables by name and identity alone, and a re-made
table keeps its name's identity (#891), so the names can't tell a head
moved to a commit holding a dropped table and then committed, a merge of
a branch still holding it, a squash of the drop into the re-make, a
table dropped and made again within one commit, or a branch made at the
old table's commit from the new one; the records can.  A commit that
isn't its working set folded (a merge's, a pick's or a revert's, over
uncommitted changes) is recorded made only from the relations its first
parent's record and the working set both have, as it holds a table
dropped and made again in the working set as the parent had it; and a
table such a move changed in the working set, uncommitted (set aside, or
left out of the commit), is recorded only as of a relation of the class
its first parent's record lists (refs' prolly_commit_local_tables, as the
merge, pick or revert tells it).  Relations are compared by OID: a move
keeps a table's relation, so a relation's history is its own.  A table a
move makes is the relation its version is of, by its OID (the canonicalizer's
move_relids_before(), asked by heap_create_with_catalog() as the table is
made, prolly_move_kept_relid()): the relation the commit it takes the
version from records under the name (prolly_move_provenance; a merge of a
table the other side made, a reset to a commit holding one dropped here),
the one a caller names (prolly_move_kept: a conflict's resolution with
theirs, the uncommitted changes put back after a move that set them
aside), or the one it was, for a table the move makes again (a definition
it can't alter in place).  Its storage is a file of its own; a relation
cache entry of that OID, of another branch's relation or of the one just
dropped, is forgotten first (RelationForgetForNewRelation()), and where it
can't be (open) or the branch has a relation of that OID, the table is a
new relation.  A branch's copies keep their OIDs, so a table is the same
relation on every branch that has it from the same making.

A table is its relation and its name together.  Two relations of one name
are two tables, whatever identity the name gives them: one dropped and
made again, one renamed into a dropped one's name, or one made on each of
two branches apart (#936).  So no move puts one relation's version into
another's table, under its grants:
- A merge (a pull, an integration, a cherry-pick, a revert, a rebase's
  step) whose other side changed (or made) a table since the base, where
  the other side's commit records it, under the name the merge matches,
  as made from another relation than the table here (or than the base's),
  keeps the table as the branch has it, with every table of its
  inheritance or partition tree (names linked through any side's
  definitions, the canonicalizer's table_parents()): base and theirs take
  those tables as ours has them (splice()), so the merge changes nothing
  in them and no row of theirs is read or listed.  Each is a table
  identity conflict (diffmerge_folded.c's identity_conflicts()), recorded
  in the merge in progress ('D', refs format 17: each table's relation
  here and theirs, its tree, its names, and the local branch theirs was
  the head of) and counted as a conflict: shown by dolt_status ("identity
  conflict"), dolt_conflicts (one each) and dolt_identity_conflicts (ours
  and theirs, relation and owner, the tree), kept by
  dolt.allow_commit_conflicts as other conflicts are, refusing a commit
  until resolved, and cleared by the merge's abort.  A fast-forward isn't
  made where theirs may bring such a version: the merge is three-way.
  A stash's apply, which has no merge in progress, and a merge that fails
  on conflicts (an integration) refuse it.
- dolt_conflicts_resolve('--ours', t) keeps the branch's relations, rows
  and grants: the conflict is resolved as the branch has it.  '--theirs'
  makes the other side's relations the branch's tables, a tree at once:
  it takes owning each of the branch's, which are dropped
  (performMultipleDeletions(), CASCADE, an object access hook watching):
  refused where the drop would take anything the move can't make again as
  the merge has it (anything outside the versioned schemas, another table
  or a column of one, a materialized view, whose query would run as the
  resolver), or an object of another role's (made again, it would be the
  resolver's: each is listed); the resolver's views, foreign keys and
  policies of versioned tables are made again, the views given back their
  grants.  The privileges of the branch's tables (their own and their
  columns') and of the views the drop took are saved in the merge in
  progress, each grant with its grantee and grantor by OID and name, and
  its abort gives them back (prolly_restore_privileges()): each grant whose
  roles are still those roles, the others (a role dropped or renamed
  since, or another role made with its name) passed over with a notice.
  Then the move makes theirs' (their definitions, indexes, constraints,
  policies and rows, from theirs' commit) as theirs' recorded owner, each
  of its OID, and gives each the privileges the local branch theirs was
  the head of has on it (branch_ops' carry_privileges(), as a merge of
  that branch gives a table it makes).  The merge's commit then records
  the chosen relations.
- (Every kind of object a move makes, re-makes or changes is treated as
  below for tables: types and domains, routines, views, sequences,
  foreign tables, versioned schemas and collations, as well as tables and
  materialized views.  A commit's record of relations lists every object
  it may hold, each with its kind and owner, named as the commit's items
  name it (the canonicalizer's prolly_owned_objects() and
  prolly_owned_routines()).  Its routines and types are stored once under
  their lines' hash (refs 'J') and named by it, so a commit that changed
  none names its parent's entry; one that changed a routine writes a new
  entry, whose value tree (cut by content) shares all but the chunks
  around the changed line with the last.  The session works out again
  only the names the caches' invalidations say may have changed: a
  routine's row changed, or a type or schema its name or arguments name
  (by the caches' hash of their OIDs), so a table or temporary table made
  works out none.  A type move's statements on the tables using a type
  run as each table's owner; each routine the conversion runs (a domain's
  check's, a cast's) runs as its own owner (below), so a type owner's code
  never gets the table owner's authority, however the type is nested (a
  domain within the table owner's composite or domain).  A domain's check
  expression itself runs as it does for each row the table's owner writes,
  as in PostgreSQL.  A cast is made and dropped as the owner of one
  of its types, as CREATE CAST requires (the role moving, if it owns one;
  else an owner whose privileges it has), and refused otherwise.)
- Every statement a move runs says, as it is made, which object it makes
  or changes, and so whose role runs it (its maker, or the role moving
  where the object is its own or no other role's); nothing is read back
  from a statement's text, and makers are found by the whole of an
  object's name, however long (a routine's names every argument).  A
  statement nothing said this of is refused, as an internal error,
  whenever the move has makers; in one that has none it runs as the role
  moving, guarded by an object access hook: one that would make, change,
  drop or empty an object of another role's whose privileges the role
  has, run such a role's routine (but an extension's as installed: owned
  by its extension's owner, the bootstrap superuser or a role with the
  mover's privileges), or make an object the move has another maker for,
  is refused, as an internal error.  So a statement the move fails to
  attribute fails closed, never runs with the mover's authority over
  another's object.
- While a move runs (its statements, row writes and checks:
  prolly_move_calls_begin()), every routine called that isn't the
  system's (an OID below FirstNormalObjectId) runs as its owner under
  SECURITY_RESTRICTED_OPERATION, as a SECURITY DEFINER routine would, with
  search_path pg_catalog, pg_temp unless it sets its own: through the
  function manager's hooks (needs_fmgr_hook, fmgr_hook), whatever path
  calls it, a statement run as a maker or as the mover, a check, default
  or index expression of the rows written, a domain's check in a
  conversion, a materialized view's query, another routine.  A routine
  whose owner has the caller's privileges (the caller's own, a
  superuser's) runs as the caller, as in PostgreSQL: running it as its
  owner would give the caller more.  A routine the hook is needed for is
  never inlined, so an SQL routine's body can't run unseen in the
  expression calling it; domains' checks the type cache planned before
  the move, and every plan the session cached (ResetPlanCache(): a
  PL/pgSQL routine's plans and simple expressions too), are planned again
  as it begins, and the plans made in it once more as it ends; and no
  query or index build of the move runs in parallel workers, which the
  hooks don't reach.  Another role's SECURITY DEFINER routine (not a
  superuser's) runs restricted too.  (A routine whose owner and caller
  have neither's privileges runs as its owner, as SECURITY DEFINER does:
  its owner's code never runs with the caller's authority.)  Function
  lookups long-lived caches made before the move aren't hooked: index
  and partition support functions in the relation cache, the type
  cache's comparison and range functions.  Those are an operator class's
  (which a superuser makes), C functions (which only a superuser can
  make), or a range type's subtype difference (its maker's choice).
- Every move that makes a table or materialized view from a commit (a
  reset, a table's checkout, a branch's first use, a rebase's end, an
  abort, a stash's push or apply, a merge, a pick, a revert, '--theirs')
  makes it as its maker: the owner the record of relations of the commit
  it takes the version from names (each line has its owner's OID and
  name), or, for uncommitted changes a move sets aside and puts back, the
  table's owner (the canonicalizer's prolly_move_maker()).  Every
  statement making it (its definition, indexes, constraints and their
  validation, policies, inheritance, the sequences it owns, a
  materialized view's query), its rows, and the checks a merge runs over
  them run as that owner under SECURITY_RESTRICTED_OPERATION, as REFRESH
  and CLUSTER run an owner's code: the role moving runs none of the
  owner's code with its own privileges, never owns the relation, and reads
  nothing of it, through it or a materialized view over it, that it wasn't
  granted.  The relation, its columns and the sequences it owns get no
  grants from their making (neither role's default privileges); a merge
  adds the source branch's grants on top (carry_privileges()).  The owner
  needs what making it takes (CREATE on its schema), as it did to make it
  first.  A recorded owner that no longer exists, or whose OID now names
  another role or a renamed one (the OID counter wrapped), refuses the
  move.  A commit with no record (fetched, cloned or pushed here) makes
  its tables as the mover, as before.  So, too, a relation the branch
  has (a table, materialized view, view or sequence) that a move changes
  in place, drops, or makes again (a table whose definition ALTER can't
  change in place, a materialized view or view made again as what it
  uses changes) is changed and made as its owner here, restricted, its
  rows written and checked so, where the role moving has that owner's
  privileges (a superuser, a member of the owning role): the owner's code
  never runs with more than the owner's own.  One made again keeps its
  owner and gets exactly its grants back (its own, its columns' and its
  sequences', a NULL ACL as well: nothing its making gave it stays).  A role without the owner's privileges moves as itself,
  as its DDL would run, refused where it doesn't own what changes (and a
  writer branch's role as its base allows: see "Writers"); whether
  row-level security refuses the rows is the mover's.  Owner-run
  statements, rows and checks use search_path pg_catalog, pg_temp
  (RestrictSearchPath()), as REFRESH does.
- A hard reset, a table's checkout from a commit and a push's or forced
  move of a branch (dolt_serve_move_head, dolt_branch -f) that would put
  into a table here a version
  of another relation (as the commit moved to records it, under the
  table's name) are refused, checked after the move and before its
  transaction can commit (VERSION_MOVE() in prolly_dolt.c, the refs'
  branch move), with a hint to drop the table here first: the move then
  makes the version's relation again, of its OID.  A move back to the
  branch's head that undoes the branch's own work (a merge's, pick's or
  revert's abort, a stash's push, a hard reset to the head) drops such
  tables itself and makes the head's relations again; so does a branch's
  first use, whose copies (of the nearest source's relations, materialized
  views over them too) may be other relations than its head's, and a
  rebase's end, the rebased commits being the branch's (where its own
  relation is the rebased head's, it stays, with its grants).  The grants
  of a table so made again went with the relation it replaced, but for an
  abort's.  A move between versions of one relation keeps it, and its
  grants.
- History reads (#932) of a table whose merge's other parent holds another
  relation of its name are refused for a SELECT grantee where they reach
  that parent's versions (dolt_history_table, dolt_diff_table and history
  mounts walk every version): the table's own first-parent versions are
  read by dolt_at, and dolt_diff_table between them.
- Moves to commits made elsewhere (fetched, cloned, pushed: no record)
  keep the relation and its grants, as before, so an old table's version
  come back from a remote can land its rows in the table here (its
  history stays refused).
A refused read reads each commit's record about once: a walk that meets
nothing marks the commits it saw apart.  Moving a head takes a write role
(see Writers), not SELECT alone.  A plan's row estimate of a version, and
dolt_diff_stat's counts, are read on the same rule.

Folded format 18 records identities in commits.  Formats are not kept
compatible while the prototype iterates: this build reads, moves to and
merges format 18 alone, and a root of format 17 or older has its tables'
rows unreadable and is refused for moves and merges ("a commit made in an
older format of prolly's history can't be ..."); commit the branch again to
write it in format 18.  A server of an earlier format refuses format 18
likewise, rather than matching renamed objects by name.


Internal DDL and event triggers
------------------------------

Checkout, reset, merge, cherry-pick, clone and other internal history work
reproduce database state without firing user event triggers.  Both SPI DDL
and direct catalog deletions use branch_writes_hush()'s existing nested,
subtransaction-aware scope.  Event-trigger dispatch and command/drop
collection ignore that scope, including when a move runs inside an outer
user DDL event.  The outer event retains its own commands and dropped
objects.  No session GUC is changed; normal user DDL still fires triggers
once the internal scope ends, including after errors.  User code that the
work runs within the scope (a constraint's check while verifying or
validating it, a materialized view's query, a trigger) is the user's: a
user-defined function called hushed is seen by the fmgr hook, and DDL it
runs fires event triggers and is collected as the user's own is.  An
extension's script, run to make the extension again, stays the work's.


Trees and chunks
----------------

A tree is an immutable sorted map identified by its root node
(prolly_tree.h).  tree_cow.c builds trees bottom up, cutting each level into
nodes with the chunker; the root is the first level with one node, so the
shape and every hash depend on the items alone.  apply() takes a sorted
batch of edits and re-chunks a window around each one, a level at a time,
until the new boundaries meet an old one; everything after that point is
shared with the old tree.  Nodes are chunks (chunkformat_basic.c) in the
database's chunk store (chunkstore_journal.c): an append-only journal,
later conjoined into table files (tablefile_basic.c), with the store's root
moved by commit records and its appends logged by the ProllyChunks WAL
resource manager (prolly_xlog.h).  A node's level is its chunk's, which
another server may have made, so every read refuses (DATA_CORRUPTED) a
node of level 31 or more (a build writes a root at 30 at most; walks keep
a frame a level), a child that isn't one level below its parent, and an
internal node with no children; and a walk refuses value trees nested
more than two deep (the walk recurses a nesting; a row's leaf names a
JSON tree, whose leaves name its long strings' value trees, and nothing
deeper names one).

The store's reads of chunks from its files (a read of the file, a CRC
check and a decompression each) go through the node cache
(prolly_nodecache.h, nodecache_shared.c): decoded chunks in a fixed area
of shared memory, prolly_node_cache_size of it, for every backend and the
fold worker, so a session's reads of trees another session or an earlier
connection read touch no file.  It is evicted by a clock, so chunks read
again and again (upper levels, the nodes repeated diffs share) stay.  An
entry is of a store as of a collection's generation: a chunk is named by
its content, so nothing kept is stale, and a read after a collection
finds nothing of what the collection removed.  Each read's level checks
run on what the cache gives as on what the files do.  pg_stat_prolly
counts its hits and misses (node_cache_hits, node_cache_misses).

Large values stay TOAST's in the working set.  A commit stores a column
whose canonical bytes are longer than 2048 as a reference to a value tree
of its own (valenc_canonical.c, through the tree's value_write()), so
rows holding the same value share it, and a row that is still long goes
out of line whole.  Value trees are cut by a rolling hash and addressed
by byte counts, so an insertion rewrites the chunks around it and a
slice reads only its own.  An edit can be spliced into a value tree
(the tree's value_splice()), giving the tree a write of the edited bytes
would: the leaves around the edit are cut again, and each level above
made again from the node on the edit's path until its nodes end where
the old ones did, so it reads and writes the nodes along that path and
the bytes around the edit, as a slice reads, whatever the value's size.
A leaf records the references its value holds, so walks reach those
trees without knowing the rows' layout.  The
value memo (memo_shared.c) maps a TOAST pointer a fold met to the
reference it made, so the next fold that meets the pointer (an UPDATE of
another column kept it) doesn't read the value again.

JSON trees.  A jsonb document whose canonical bytes are longer than 2048
is stored by content, and folded, as a JSON tree (prolly_jsontree.h,
jsontree_paths.c) rather than a value tree of its bytes: a tree of the
tree interface keyed by paths, the keys' bytes sorting as jsonb orders
the document.  A container of 1024 bytes or fewer is one item holding its
jsonb bytes; a longer one is its children's items, each scalar an item.
The tree is built from the canonical bytes (strings in UTF-8), so its
root is a function of the document: equal documents share it however
they were made, and a fold takes the tree from the row's hash pointer
unread.  A document that can't be a tree (a scalar, or a path too long
for a key) is a value tree of its bytes.  A row's reference to a JSON
tree records length 0, which no value tree out of line has.  A whole read
lays the items out as jsonb lays a document out, a packed container's
bytes copied as they are, checks the result as canonical jsonb bytes are
checked, and converts it to the database's encoding; it holds the
document's children as small records and their bytes in one arena, so it
takes a bounded multiple of the document's size however many containers
it has.  A tree may come from another server: every other reader checks
a packed container's bytes before parsing them.  Decoding a row
whose tree is in the database's store gives a hash pointer to it, so
history (dolt_at(), snapshot mounts) and the rows a checkout, reset or merge
writes back name the tree unread; dolt_history_table()'s composite rows
read it.  prolly_jsonb.c's SQL functions take the value as stored:
prolly_jsonb_get() reads only the items under a path, prolly_jsonb_set()
replaces the items under it (a packed container's edited by jsonb_set())
and packs the containers above it that have become short enough (an
UPDATE then writes the nodes on that path, not the document; a result
short enough to be inline comes back whole), and prolly_jsonb_diff()
diffs two trees by their nodes; anything a tree doesn't handle, any
document that isn't one, and every document in a database whose encoding
isn't UTF-8, they do whole, as #>, jsonb_set() and a diff of the
documents would.  jsonb's own ->, ->>, #>, #>>, subscripts (read and
assigned), ?, ?|, ?&, #-, jsonb_set(), jsonb_set_lax() and jsonb_insert()
do the same before they detoast their argument (prolly_jsonb.h): -> by a
key only on an object and by an index only on an array, ? and its kin by
the keys' paths on an object and by reading an array's elements in order
(skipping what a container element holds), and prolly_jsonb_set() is
jsonb_set() itself.  #-, jsonb_insert() and a subscript's assignment go
through the tree's edit(): it runs the jsonb function itself on a
document of only what is along the path (so its errors are the
function's), and puts back what it made of the item there; an edit that
would move an array's other elements is done whole.  An edit's nodes are the top
transaction's chunks, so it is done whole in a parallel operation, a
subtransaction, a read-only transaction and recovery; a result no row
takes leaves its nodes for the chunk store's collection.  Other jsonb
functions read
the document whole.  A negative index counts from the array's end, its
size read from its last element's key.  An array's indexes are in its
elements' keys, so an edit only replaces an element of an array that
isn't packed: an insert or a removal, which would renumber every element
after it, is done whole, unless it is at the array's end (an element
appended, the last one removed).  Folded format 22 is the first whose rows hold JSON
trees.

A hash pointer a read hands out (a long value of history, read or not)
names chunks a collection may drop: the chunk store's keep() is told of
it, and a collection keeps the chunk or waits for the transaction, read
only or not.  A hot standby's transaction has no transaction id to be
waited for, and the collections are the primary's, replayed: keep() notes
it (its virtual transaction) as holding pointers, and the replay of a
swap cancels each holder of the store's, after
max_standby_streaming_delay, as a recovery conflict on the chunk store
(pg_stat_database_conflicts.confl_chunk_store), before the replayed
manifest stops naming what it may hold.  Every holder is cancelled,
whether or not the collection drops what it holds, and
hot_standby_feedback doesn't prevent it: the primary's collection waits
for no standby.  A holder's own keep() during the replay goes on (the
replay is waiting for it), but anyone else's waits for the swap to be
replayed (ProllySwapReplay) and then fails if its chunk is gone: while the
replay waits out the holders, up to max_standby_streaming_delay (with -1,
until they end), new history reads of long values in that database stall.
One begun on a standby and still running at its promotion stays noted,
and the new primary's collections wait for it.  So history reads assign
no transaction id, and a hot standby serves them as its primary does.


Writers
-------

Every write to a branch is noted before it is made (branch_note_write(),
catalog/branch.h): rows of prolly tables, versioned sequences, and
versioned objects' catalog rows, unless made on the session's behalf by
a version-control call.  The writers ("modes", writers_modes.c) decide
whether the write may go ahead.  In the shared mode every session writes
every branch, as in Dolt.  In the owned mode a dirty branch is one
writer's (dolt.writer, by default the session): its pg_branch row records
the owner, claimed by the first write and forgotten once a commit or
reset leaves the branch clean, and anyone else's write is refused.  A
protected branch (main in the branch-only mode, or any branch protected
by name) has no writers: only integrations change it, a merge,
cherry-pick, revert, reset or commit that commits as it changes it, as
dolt_integrate merges a writer's branch in.  In the branch-auto mode a
statement that writes, at the top level, on a protected branch first
moves the session to its writer branch (writer/<writer>/<base>), forked
from the base as the transaction's snapshot saw it and swept once it is
clean and merged.  The mode, protections and writer branches are
database settings kept in the refs.

Moving a branch's head is a write to the branch, whatever its tables'
grants: a commit (dolt_commit, or dolt.transaction_commit's at a
transaction's end), a reset of any kind (a hard reset to the head too, as
it drops the branch's changes), a merge or pull into it, a cherry-pick,
revert or rebase onto it, an integration (of the branch, and into its
target), and the branch's deletion, rename or forced move
(dolt_branch('-f', ...)).  Each takes prolly_check_head_move(): the role
has the privileges of the branch's owner, or writes the refs at large
(prolly_writes_refs(): it owns the database, has the database's CREATE
privilege, which makes branches, or has the privileges of
pg_prolly_write).  A branch's owner is the role that made it, recorded in
the refs ('A', refs format 14) as it is made, its first head set by a
fetch, clone or push included, kept by a rename and by a forced move that
makes a branch without commits again; main and a branch whose maker isn't
recorded are the database owner's.  dolt_branches shows it (creator).
Each maker the refs record (a branch's, a tag's, a stash's) is a shared
dependency of its role in pg_shdepend, as an object's owner is: the
record keeps the role's OID and an id of its own (newMadeObjectId()),
which the entry names it by (classid RefsBranchClassId, RefsTagClassId or
RefsStashClassId), and the entry is recorded, moved and dropped with the
record, in the same transaction.  So DROP ROLE refuses a role that still
makes any and lists them, REASSIGN OWNED makes the new role their maker,
and DROP OWNED deletes the role's tags and stashes but gives its branches
to the database's owner (their maker no longer recorded): deleting a
branch would lose the commits no other branch has, and the working set
every session on it shares.  No maker record outlives its role, so a
role later given a dropped role's OID inherits nothing.  A writer
branch's record of the role that forked it (in the writer modes' config)
is the one exception, a bare OID checked only for existing; it grants
nothing alone, since a writer's integration also moves its writer
branch's head, which takes the branch's owner, its maker: a writer
branch reassigned is no longer the old writer's to integrate, and one
given up is no writer's.  The refs
lock is held while REASSIGN OWNED and DROP OWNED find each object by its
record's id, so a concurrent push onto a stash list or a rename can't
redirect them.  DROP ROLE's hint for objects in another database names
REASSIGN OWNED and DROP OWNED there.  A rename's new name takes what making a branch of it takes, or
pg_prolly_write's privileges, and a rebase's own branch isn't renamed: a
rebase's branch and a writer branch are made on the session's behalf,
without the privilege to make branches, and are its role's.  In the
writer modes a writer with only its tables' grants writes and commits on
its writer branch, and integrates its own writer branch into its base if
every change the branch brings is to rows of tables it may write as they
changed (INSERT for rows added, DELETE for rows removed, UPDATE for rows
changed, as the base's catalogs grant them; the move that applies them
takes all four of a table's write privileges, as every move does).  A
change to a definition, or to rows it may not write so, and an
integration of any other branch, take the head move's privileges.

A move drops and makes again every table's policies and makes each
definition it changes, as the session's role.  On a writer branch, a move
whose every definition change makes an object what the writer base's head
defines (a reset to the base, a merge of it, an integration's
fast-forward) runs those statements as the least role that may make
them, restricted (SECURITY_RESTRICTED_OPERATION): the owner of what it
changes or drops, if they are one role, and the database's owner for what
it makes; else (several owners, or a kind whose owner isn't looked up)
the bootstrap superuser.  Otherwise a policy statement runs as its
table's owner when the move leaves the table's policies as they are, or
makes them the base's.  This rests on a move running no user code: its
statements are deparsed definitions, run with check_function_bodies off,
and no trigger or event trigger fires for its own DDL (internal history
work's); the rows it writes go as the session's role, under its
privileges (a table or materialized view it makes from a commit is made,
and its rows written, as its recorded owner, restricted: see "Identity
across renames" above).  A writer with its tables' grants alone so moves to what its
base's writers defined, never to definitions of its own choosing on
objects it doesn't own.

Commits hold no privileges, so a relation a move makes has only the ones
its making gives it.  A merge (or fast-forward) of a branch of this
database, though, gives each relation it makes on the target, one new to
it (not one made again in the place of one of its name the move dropped),
the privileges of the source branch's relation it was made from: the
relation's, and each column's by name, as the new relation's owner (its
maker, the owner its commit records, or the merging role for a commit
with no record) gives them, with their shared dependencies (branch_ops'
carry_privileges); a relation with a maker has no others, its making
giving it none.  The source relation's owner, if another role, keeps its
own, as a grant to it from the new owner.  The merging role approves them as it
approves the table.  The relations the move makes and drops are found by
the object access hook as it runs, so this costs only as much as the move
changes.

Commits hold no planner statistics either.  A branch's first use copies its
source's relations with theirs, and a reset or restore keeps a table's own,
but a table a merge or a pick of a branch's head makes on the target is
loaded from rows, its index builds counting them and no column analyzed:
until autovacuum analyzes it, the planner guesses, and joins of such tables
can get nested-loop plans that run for minutes.  So each table such a move
makes, as above, takes the source relation's statistics (branch_ops'
carry_statistics): its columns' pg_statistic rows (a partitioned table's
inherited ones too), its expression indexes' (from the source's index of
the same name and expressions), its extended statistics objects' data (from
the source's of the same name and kinds over the same columns by number:
not where a dropped column puts them at other numbers on one side), and its
row and page counts if it has none.  A column's are carried to the column of
the same name and collation whose type is the same by identity: the same
shared type, or a versioned one the move made again with another OID, an
enum of the same name and labels in the same order (its values, label OIDs,
mapped label to label) or a domain of the same name over the same shared
base type, or an array of either (its most common elements and its whole
arrays carried as their elements are).  A statistic whose values are of
another type than the column's (an array column's elements, a range's
lengths) is copied as it is when that type is shared.  Columns of other
versioned types (composites and table row types, ranges, arrays of those,
and a domain over an array of a versioned type) hold the source's OIDs in
their values and aren't carried (the table's other columns are), nor are
extended statistics on expressions or over columns of versioned types.
Nothing is carried for a table whose live columns the source's working set
has renamed, retyped, added or dropped since its head: its statistics could
describe other columns than the ones of their names here.  Its rows may have
changed since its head too, so the statistics are an estimate, as they lag
anyway; a table the source never analyzed takes none.  A
table a reset, a checkout of a commit or a pull makes has no relation of a
branch known to hold its rows, and waits for autovacuum.

The source's relation is the one the branch's records of its head's
relations list under the name (quoted and escaped as commits' records write
names): its tables' ('N', the record its commits' records are made
from) and its other relations' with privileges of their own ('H': views,
sequences, partitioned and foreign tables), both written by each of its
commits, and only while the branch's head is their commit.  One of the
name made since, uncommitted (another relation, perhaps of another
kind), gives nothing, nor does a branch whose head moved without a
commit (a fast-forward, a reset).  A branch made from another starts
with both records, as with its relations, of the same OIDs: its first
commit's record so keeps what its start point's had, as a later
commit's keeps its parent's.  dolt_integrate()
merges so too.  A cherry-pick of one ref that names a branch (so picks
its head) takes that branch's privileges alone; one of a commit named
otherwise (a hash, HEAD~n, several refs) takes none, since the commit
may be older than any branch's head, whose relation of the name may be
another made since, and no one branch is its source.  A revert takes
none: what it makes again, its commit dropped.  A relation merged from
a remote's ref (a fetch, a pull), a tag or a commit arrives with only
those its making gives it, as does one a reset makes.  A merge,
cherry-pick, revert or stash apply runs between prolly_move_begin()
and prolly_move_end(); inside it (from a function the rows it writes
call), nothing that moves a head or the working set runs: another of
them, a reset, a checkout, a commit (prolly_check_not_moving(), which
prolly_check_head_move() calls too) is refused.  Row security and policies are held in commits, so they arrive
with the relation whatever its source.

Other shared state in the refs is guarded the same way.  Making a tag, and
adding or removing a remote, takes prolly_writes_refs(); deleting a tag
takes that or having made it ('G', its maker, recorded by dolt_tag()
alone: a tag fetched or pushed is no one's).  A fetch sets the remote's
tags here only for a role that writes the refs; another's fetch leaves
them, with a notice.  A stash records the role
that pushed it; dropping or popping it takes that role's privileges or
prolly_writes_refs(), and a clear by a role without the latter clears only
its own.  dolt_serve_move_head(), a push from another database, takes
pg_prolly_push's privileges instead; deleting a branch through it is a
deletion, and takes these.

Automatic forks happen before parse analysis (including modifying CTEs)
and before cached statements reacquire relation locks. Creators therefore
wait for refs without holding write locks on the protected source that
another creator needs to copy. Analysis and cached-plan revalidation use
the writer branch's catalogs. Cleanup rechecks its candidates under refs,
so concurrent checkouts cannot delete the same completed writer twice.


Commits
-------

Table keying (keying_pkprefix.c) keys a folded table by its primary key,
or, without one, by its rows' leading bytes and content hash with a count
of identical rows.  A commit folds each table's rows into its data tree:
in full the first time, then from its baseline using the per-table change
log (the default finder "log", changes_log.c). The older "pages" finder
(changes_pages.c) remains registered. Incremental folds can fall back to a
full fold when the log or baseline cannot be used, or when more rows
changed than reading the table costs.  A table whose rows' bytes changed
shape without being written (a column added with a default, one dropped,
the key moved) is folded from its baseline's rows mapped to the shape now
only where that costs less than reading it: both read every row, and
decoding the baseline's costs more per row than scanning the heap's until
the heap holds about prolly.map_reshaped_dead_ratio (1.5, a rough
break-even) dead versions a live row, as its statistics count them (which
lag, and count none after a VACUUM or on a standby: a wrong choice costs
only time, the tree made being the same).  dolt_pending_full_folds() asks
the same.  Changes past maintenance_work_mem
are not a reason: they are sorted, spilling to disk, and applied a part at
a time in key order, so the fold's work stays that of the rows changed
(pg_stat_prolly's fold_changes_spilled).  A transaction commit's own
changes past it commit the working set instead.
The refs ("copies", refs_copies.c) keep branch heads, baselines, tags, remotes and
merge state in the chunk store's root.  Diff and merge ("folded",
diffmerge_folded.c) compare canonical trees table by table, reading only
subtrees that differ; moving a branch to another commit writes the
differences back through the row applier (applier_replication.c), as
logical replication's apply worker writes rows.  A commit of a canonical
state a caller already holds is written by the refs' commit_root(), from
the root, dependencies, parents and metadata given: it folds nothing,
reads no catalog for the commit and moves no branch.  The caller names the
commit in the same transaction: a collection after it ends drops a commit
no ref names.  commit_with() folds
the working set (and the dependencies, from the catalogs) and writes its
commit through it.

Walking history (HEAD~n, ^n, a commit named by its hash) takes each
commit's links (its root, parents and height) from the commit cache
(prolly_commitcache.h; "shared", commitcache_shared.c), a table in shared
memory of prolly_commit_cache_entries commits (4096; 0 turns it off),
before reading the commit.  The node cache lets go of the commits as soon
as a large diff or reset reads past them, so without it each step of a
walk could be a read from disk.  An entry is of the chunk
store's GC generation it was read in (the chunk store's generation()) and
is found only in that generation, so a collection's swap lets go of them
all; dropping a database forgets its entries.  A transaction's entries
are everyone's only once it commits (pg_stat_prolly's commit_cache_hits
and commit_cache_misses).

Each branch has a head log (the refs' head_at() and head_moves()): its
head's moves in order, each with when it was made and the commit it moved
to (a commit, a merge, a reset, a transaction's commit, a pull's
fast-forward), with the transaction; a remote-tracking branch has one as
fetches and pushes move it.  "The commit branch B was at at time T" is a
seek (prolly_head_at(), prolly_head_log()), whatever dates the commits
were given.  A log is a tree of its own, keyed by each move's date and
number, so a move goes at its right edge; the latest moves (at most 16)
wait in the branch's mover entry, which every move writes anyway, and go
into the tree together, so a commit doesn't rewrite more of the refs for
its log.  A log goes with its branch (a rename keeps it).  "The nearest
commit at or before commit C" is no question of a log: walk C's parents.

A table both sides of a merge changed in ways that don't merge (its
definitions changed differently, or one dropped it and the other changed
it, its definition or only its rows) is a schema conflict.  dolt_merge()
keeps it as the branch has it, definition and rows, records it in the
merge in progress (the refs' merge names entry, OID 0 and its names on
each side: refs format 12) and merges the other tables, as Dolt does;
dolt_schema_conflicts shows its statements in the base, the head and
theirs, each under its own name.  A table a side renamed (matched by
identity) is kept under ours' names, its own and its columns': the
merge's names are made again so (merge_renames()'s keep_ours), found by
merging the definitions once before the merge does.
dolt_conflicts_resolve() refuses it, and the merge can't be committed
until it is aborted.  A table the other side has as another relation is a
table identity conflict instead (see "Identity across renames").  A
cherry-pick or revert, schema conflicts of other objects, and a table the
other side's objects use in its version (a view over a column it added)
fail as before, the last saying so.

A keyed row both sides of a merge changed is merged column by column, on
its encoded fields (the keying's row_fields()): all three versions are
rows of the table as defined now (a version of another shape is converted
first), so a column's bytes name its value.  A column whose bytes one side
left as base's takes the other's, one both changed alike keeps it, and the
merged row is assembled from the fields taken (encode_row_fields()), long
values by their references, no column decoded.  A large value neither side
changed is not fetched to find that out, nor when the merged row is
applied.  A row with a column whose base value is unsure (below) is
decoded and compared by value instead, except that where a side's long
value has base's reference neither is read (decode_row_kept()) and the
merged row keeps the reference (encode_row_kept()).  An assert-enabled
build merges each row both ways and checks they agree.

When a type change admits both text and cast readings of the base, merge
validates both interpretations before applying rows.  Each outcome walk
spools its ordered keys, conflict flags and canonical values to a temporary
BufFile.  Comparison holds one outcome from each stream and the unchanged
row, rather than two complete lists.  Outcome memory is two file buffers
plus reusable buffers sized to the largest records (and BufFile segment
metadata); temporary I/O grows with the outcomes.  Files close on success
and their resource owner removes them on error, savepoint rollback or
cancellation.  Ordinary merges do not create these files, but every such
validation creates both, however small the table, so it uses temporary
space: it counts against temp_file_limit and is reported by log_temp_files.
Reading a version as a table is defined now (as_defined_now()) keeps the
rows in their stored order when the key columns are read as they are
stored.  Otherwise (a key retyped, an enum relabelled, a keyless table) the
converted rows go to a tuplesort of their own ordered by the keying, ties
in the order read, which spills past work_mem; they are read back in order
and streamed into the tree builder, copies of a row counted and a key found
twice refused or, shown, warned of, as before.  The base's rows with an
unsure value are sorted the same way and written to a temporary file in key
order, which each merge walk reads from its start alongside theirs' changes.
While a version's rows are read, the converted rows' sort and the unsure
rows' sort can both be open, each with its own work_mem: the conversion
uses up to twice work_mem, plus each sort's merge buffers.

A merge that records constraint violations writes each table's rows as an
attempt.  Where a refused row's put-back is refused too, the table's writes
are rolled back and made again; the violations an attempt records are
spooled to a temporary file of its own, dropped when the attempt is rolled
back.  When it holds, those left are read back in order into the merge's
kept violations, passing over the handles of those forgotten (a row tried
again and written after all), which are sorted.  The rows ranked up across
attempts, and those held to be tried again while a row is pinned, are
spooled to temporary files in key order.  These files are created under the
resource owner the writes began with so that an attempt's subtransaction
can roll back without closing them; their memory is a few file buffers and
the largest row.  A keyless table's removed values, which the domain checks
look up, are held in memory up to work_mem, then sorted with a tuplesort
and written once each, in order, to an image file and a fixed-size entry
file that lookups search by halving.  All of these files close when the
table is written and go with their resource owner on error or cancellation.

A row a merge didn't write for breaking a unique index is recorded as
Dolt records it: once for each unique index it breaks, and so is each
row of the table it collides with there (the table keeping those).
Those kept are spooled to a tuplesort of their own; once the merge's
rows are written, they are matched a batch at a time, by a query per
unique index of plain columns (its predicate, if any, held by both rows;
each column compared by the index's own equality and collation, so the
index serves the lookup), against the rows
they collide with, as the merge's other checks run (seeing its writes,
as the table's owner, row security aside: an owner who can't use the
table's schema fails the merge, as its foreign key checks do), and the
violations found are kept as any other.  A refused row is looked up as
it was, read back from its row type's text kept with it (floats in
full), not from its JSON, which loses a jsonb null, an array's bounds
and a float's digits.  With NULLs not distinct, each pattern of the
refused row's NULLs (up to four key columns) is a lookup of its own, so
the index serves those too.  A refused row colliding with none now, or refused
for an index of expressions, keeps its refusal's own record.

The violations a merge keeps go to a tuplesort of their own (work_mem,
spilling past it).  To publish them, the branch's violations are visited
into the same sort, which orders them by what they are and then the
branch's first: the first of each is kept only if it is new, and the new
ones are sorted again into the order found and added to the branch's a
batch at a time (1000, or work_mem's worth of bytes up to 1MB).  A merge or
pick counts the branch's violations, to report how many it added, by
visiting them, not loading them.  The refs keep a branch's violations one entry each, keyed
by the branch and a place, after an entry holding the next place, so adding
some reads and writes none of the others (the refs' format version 2).
Format version 1 kept them all in one value under the branch's key; this
build doesn't read such a value but refuses it, with a hint to reset the
branch, which removes it.  The violations' inspection functions and the
checks before a commit still read a branch's violations whole.

The constraint checks (a merge's foreign keys, the check and not-null rows
a merge's added constraints take away, dolt_verify_constraints()) run each
query through SPI_execute_extended() with a DestReceiver that hands each
row found to its consumer as the executor returns it: the merge's
violation sort, or a list for verification, which sets the branch's
violations whole.  No query's result is held as an SPI tuple table.  The
keys of the rows changed since the head, which verification matches a
batch at a time, go to two stores shared by every table (keys now, and as
they were), each held in memory up to half of work_mem and then moved to
a temporary file; a table's keys of a side are a segment of its store, as
the diff gives a table's rows together.  What reading a table's rows takes
is released before the next table's, and each batch's JSON literal and
query are made only as that batch runs.  A merge still checks every foreign key of the tables it
wrote whole, by query; the executor's own sorts and hashes spill under
work_mem as for any query.

A commit, a checkpoint and a fold of a transaction's own rows change
only their branch's refs (its head, baseline, the names of its head's
tables, its merge state and violations, the commits its pages are kept
as of).  Every change of a branch's refs
is made holding that branch's lock to transaction end, and they take
only that, so commits on different branches run together while those on
one branch take turns.  Anything else that changes the refs (a branch
made, deleted or renamed, a reset, a merge, a push) holds the session
branch's lock (and any branch's it changes), then moves the store's root
with its transaction, as all of them did before: other such movers wait
for it.  A branch's holder makes its changes in a refs tree of its own,
rebuilt on the store's root when another's commit moved it, and moves the
root there as it commits, waiting then for a mover holding it; one that
comes to change other refs moves the root from then on.  So an operation
reading another branch's head (a branch made from it, a merge of it)
reads the head as last committed, not a commit still open there.

A move (or a first use's move of its copies) gives each table whose rows
differ from the target's the storage of another state that holds the
target's table, where sharing costs less than writing the rows (branch
operations' share(), the canonicalizer's splice_baseline()); a branch's
first use copies the branch nearest its head.  A source is a branch whose
working set is known (folded, nobody writing it since), or a kept
materialization (matcache_branches.c): the hidden branch of a detached
checkout, kept once its sessions leave, which nothing writes again.  Its
root must hold the target's table, the same data tree and description,
and its relations must be the same ones (by id) stored alike: the same
access method, columns as stored (dropped ones, and the values a short
tuple's missing columns read as, included), TOAST table and index
definitions.  Sharing a tree-mapped relation copies its root page, so its
cost doesn't grow with the table; the source is held against deletion to
the transaction's end.  A kept source is used only by a snapshot that sees
it made, so every tuple on its pages is one the snapshot sees as committed
or aborted, and only while its commit's ordering dependencies match the
installed extensions, under which its indexes were built.  pg_stat_prolly
counts the tables a move gave another state's storage (move_tables_shared)
and the tables and materialized views whose rows it wrote
(move_tables_written): a checkout's work,
table by table.

None of these lists every branch, so their cost doesn't grow with the
number of branches.  The refs keep indexes, changed in the same
transactions as the entries they index: of kept commits by commit and by
each kept table's data tree, of branches by their heads' roots and their
baselines' folds' roots, and of branches' names case folded.  A move's
known sources are the branches the generation table knows a fold of (a
table of fixed size); its kept sources are the kept checkouts (the hidden
branches' prefix of pg_branch's name index) and the kept commits holding
one of the target's data trees.  A first use's nearest branch is ranked
among the branches at the head's root by those indexes and the known
folds; the planner's candidates are listed in name order only until
enough are found; branch making checks the name folded with one seek.
pg_stat_prolly's branches_listed counts the branches listings visit.

With no branch at its head, the move planner (moveplan_costs.c)
costs moving each branch's copies there, from the root its working set is
known or thought to be: each table's rows that differ, estimated from its
trees by diff_estimate() in a bounded number of nodes, and the tables
redefined, made or dropped (materialized views' rows as tables'), each
planned on its own branch.  Where the nodes that differ don't fit the
bound, the estimate's walk stops above them and walks a sample of the key
ranges they cover down (64 at most, taken in proportion to their rows, so
a range appended, deleted or rewritten whole among many small ones is
taken), scaling the changes it finds for each row sampled to the rows of
the ranges not taken, and never counting fewer there than the rows one
side holds and the other doesn't.  How many nodes differ can't
tell a few changes spread over a table from as many runs of rows
rewritten (a thousand rows updated across a million leave a thousand
leaves differing, and nearly every node above them, as a thousand runs
of a leaf's worth would); the changes a sampled range holds for each of
its rows can.  (Counting every row under the nodes unread, as it once
did, took a few spread changes for most of the table, and reloaded it,
every row written and then folded in full, instead of moving its few.)  The session's branch and at most 16 others are
weighed, each stopped once it costs more than the best yet; the cheapest is
copied, the session's on a tie or near one (another must cost a fifth less,
the estimates being a sample's).  Another branch is copied only if none of
its writers is in the middle of a change (make()'s nowait): waiting for one,
with the refs held, would hold up the session's branch's commits for as long
as that writer's transaction lasts.  Else the session's branch is copied,
waiting for its writers, as ever (pg_stat_prolly's first_use_sources_busy).
The copies are new in the transaction, so no other sees them: a table of the chosen plan whose rows differ by more
than reloading it costs (past about an eighth of them) begins empty instead
(branch operations' make(), its emptied tables: new storage, as TRUNCATE
gives it, no page shared, its change log told), and the move loads the
head's rows into it, its indexes built once after them (the row applier's
load).  Not a table with a deferrable unique, primary key or exclusion
constraint, whose rows the applier rechecks a row at a time.  A table the
head hasn't begins empty too, so the move drops it without its pages
having been shared (and, for pages no other map held, logged) first.  A
table whose definition differs keeps its pages: the move may keep its rows.

A branch's pages are kept as of the last commits it was at
(prolly.kept_commits, 2 by default): when a commit's fold, or a move
settled, leaves the working set known to be a commit's root, each
block-mapped relation stored with its tables (heaps, indexes, TOAST tables,
change logs) is kept by a snapshot of its storage (bmap_snapshot_take(),
through branch operations' keep()), O(1) in its size, a relation unchanged
since a commit kept already reusing that one's.  A snapshot writes out only
the relation's pages changed since its last snapshot (those its map walk
finds), never visiting the buffers.  Redo of one writes the same pages,
walking the snapshot's own leaves as the record names them, and syncs
nothing: those pages were logged as images (below).

Durability through WAL.  The blocks a share or snapshot comes to share
must hold its pages durably, as long as anything holds them: they never
change in place again, and recovery that finds a newer map skips the
records of a page from before it moved off them.  WAL makes them so, at
every wal_level: each own block
is logged as an image (PRESERVE) before the share's or snapshot's record,
which says so, and its write (still needed: a relation made of the share
reads the blocks) registers the block's segment for the checkpointer's
sync like any page's.  Recovery from a checkpoint before an image replays
it to the block, torn or lost writes included; one from a later
checkpoint finds the block written and synced by it (a page dirty as a
checkpoint begins is written by it, and every write registered before
its sync phase is synced).  WAL holding an image is let go of only past
such a checkpoint, a commit relying on the share flushes WAL past its
images (with synchronous_commit off, a crash may lose the commit, but WAL
is replayed as a prefix: no commit survives without the images before
it), and a base backup either replays the image or copies the block
after its start checkpoint wrote and synced it.  Redo of such a record
syncs nothing (the images' redo wrote each block, and a restartpoint
syncs it), so a standby, or a promoted one, follows the same argument.
It still writes the relation's pages out, as the primary does: a page
found the relation's own before the share or snapshot may change in place
after its image and before the record (a row lock or a prune, which don't
ask the map), and that change's redo reaches the relation's buffer only,
which nothing else would write to the block before a restartpoint.
Redo goes by the record's flag, not the current wal_level, which can
change only across a restart, whose shutdown checkpoint syncs everything.

Syncing the page store instead, at wal_level minimal, isn't enough: a
crash's replay may write the block again after the sync made it durable.
The record that gave the block to its relation zeroes it again (its
extension), an earlier holder's images and records put their pages there
(one dropped after the redo point), a shrink of the store cuts it, and the
relation's own records that made the page are skipped once its map was
dropped (a hard reset replacing its storage) or the page moved off the
block.  Only an image logged after them all restores it.  For the same
reason block-mapped storage made in a transaction at minimal, whose
writes skip WAL, has its pages logged as images at commit whatever its
size, never only synced (see "Skipping WAL for New RelFileLocator" in
access/transam/README): a sync before a crash doesn't survive the replay
of the block's earlier records.

A share with no block of its own (a table just restored or shared, or
unchanged since its last snapshot: every block held by another map or
snapshot already) skips its flush when no flush is in flight.  Its blocks
were each written out by the share or snapshot that first shared them
(their own blocks then) and can't change in place since (a change copies
a shared page first, and only a hint reaches its buffer).  Shared memory
follows each store's flushes of own blocks: begun before the blocks'
counts are raised (under the relation's header, so whoever finds them
shared finds the flush in flight or written), and written.  One given up
before its write was done (an error part way) may have left its pages in
their buffers alone, and leaves the store not written out until a
checkpoint begun after it completes.  At most 64 stores are followed at
once: past that, the least recently used idle one is let go of.

Redo of a snapshot visits every buffer
of the relation instead when it can't trust a page the walk reads: with
data checksums off (a torn page would go unseen), or at a map or count
page newer than the replay (a crash's, or a base backup's), one that
fails verification, one past its file, or one not the node its pointer
names (a node freed since and its block taken as a relation's page).
Only while nothing else can be on the pages: the relations are locked
against writers without waiting, and the snapshot the commit or move went
by saw every writer of the branch that ended and nobody else is writing
it; otherwise nothing is kept.  Not at a transaction's own commit
(dolt.transaction_commit), whose head moves every transaction.  The refs
record each kept commit with the branch's baseline then and each table's
relations and snapshots ('K').
A snapshot's slot names the transaction that took it, so one whose
transaction doesn't commit (a crash between the keep and the commit
included) is kept by nobody: the relation's next snapshot, or a page
sweep, lets go of it.  VACUUM leaves the pages a relation shares only
with its own snapshots as it leaves any shared page with nothing to
reclaim (an aggressive VACUUM still freezes them, copying them).  With
prolly.vacuum_copy_kept_pages on, it freezes and sets them all-visible,
copying them (the kept version goes once its commit stops being kept),
but still leaves as they are the pages another branch shares, which the
block map tells apart by when each page's entry was born (its header's
shared_below).  A share
raises shared_below; once the other branch's map is let go of, a page
sweep (prolly_page_sweep()), having counted every holder, lowers it to
the newest entry another map still holds, so VACUUM treats those pages
as unshared again.  It holds each such relation's branch meanwhile, as a
session on it does, so a branch's deletion or rename tried then fails as
if a session were on it (its detail says either may be).  A
move back to one of them (dolt_reset('--hard') to the head or the commit
before, a merge's abort, a stash) first gives each table that changed
since its pages back (restore()): new storage made of each relation's
snapshot, O(1), its pg_class row repointed with its oldest transaction ids
then, as TRUNCATE gives a relation new storage, and the old storage kept
for the snapshots older than the move (below).  The branch's other kept
commits' snapshots of the relation were in the old storage: each is carried into the new one (bmap_snapshot_carry(),
a slot holding the same subtrees, each a holder more, O(1)), and their
entries renamed, so those commits stay kept and resets back and forth
between kept commits restore every time.  A carry's record (a snapshot
record flagged BMAP_TREE_CARRIED, without the header) is redone writing
and syncing nothing: its blocks are the original snapshot's, written out
when that was kept.  An abort or a crash before the commit drops or
orphans the new storage, slots and all; the old one is untouched.  A
move that gives a table the pages another of the branch's own kept
commits holds of the target's rows (share_kept(), from a commit with the
same data: an empty commit's, an amend's) carries them the same way.  A
move that gives a table another branch's storage (share(), or
share_kept() of another branch's kept pages) replaces the table's storage
too but carries nothing: the other storage's epochs say nothing of this
branch's snapshots, so the branch's kept commits of that table are
dropped (kept_drop_dead()), and a reset back to one writes its rows.  The
baseline takes the table's entry from the kept baseline,
whose change log is restored with it, so the fold that follows reads none
of its rows.  A move to a commit whose pages aren't kept, or whose kept
pages for a table are gone, gives each table that changed since the head
the pages kept as of the branch's head instead, whatever the working set
holds: that discards the working set's changes in O(1), as a reset to
the head does, and the move then writes only the rows the head and the
commit differ by.  How far the working set is from the commit isn't
asked: telling it without folding could only bound it (from the
baseline and the change finder's count since it), and a working set the
fold worker had folded only part of would be taken for one near the
commit, and folded and written back, every row of it, where the head's
pages discard it whatever its size.  A working set brought near the
commit by its own writes so costs the head's rows from the commit, the
distance a reset from a clean working set writes too.  Other sessions'
snapshots don't hold it back either: the storage a restore replaces is
kept (below), and a snapshot older than the move goes on reading it.  A
transaction elsewhere whose snapshot can't move (REPEATABLE READ,
SERIALIZABLE) may come to the branch later, by a checkout or a read of
its working set: the move records that it gave the branch its head's
pages (in shared memory: no snapshot outlives a restart), and such a
transaction, if its snapshot doesn't see the move, fails with a
serialization failure instead.  A branch reset back and forth between two commits
takes this path: each restore gives a table new storage, and the other
commit's snapshot goes with the old storage.  Whatever still differs
(definitions, sequences, a table not restored) the move writes as before.
A table isn't restored if its
storage was replaced since (a rewrite, a truncation), an index was made or
dropped, it was renamed, its rows' transaction ids are older than the
database still knows, its rows in the commit can't be read here, the user
couldn't write its rows, or the session itself is using one of its
relations (a scan of it open, an AFTER trigger's event queued): its rows
are written.  A hard reset takes its restores' locks, as TRUNCATE takes
them, waiting for them, before it holds the refs (lock_for_restore():
the tables restore_pages() will give pages, planned as it plans them, so
not one whose rows it writes, as a reset of a few rows does): giving up
would leave it every row the working set changed to write back, however
many, and waiting while it held the refs would hold up every other
branch's operation behind a reader of one table.  Every other operation
takes the branch's refs, then the root, then the relations it reads or
writes; so the reset takes its tables' locks all or none, waiting for
the first busy one with nothing else held and then trying all again,
and then only tries the refs and the root: if either is held, it gives
every table's lock back and waits in each one's queue, holding nothing
(taking it and letting go of it at once, so the deadlock detector and
lock_timeout see the wait), and begins again.  Planning takes no lock it
keeps (what it opens to read, it lets go of), so two resets of one branch
at once never each hold a table the other waits for.  It never holds a lock another operation waits for
while it waits for one that operation holds.  A page sweep holding a
table's lock lets go of it for a waiter, an autovacuum worker is
cancelled after deadlock_timeout (as for any waiter), the deadlock
detector sees the wait, and lock_timeout bounds it, failing the reset.
Any other restore's locks (another move's, a reset's in a transaction
holding the refs already), and a share's, are tried, not queued for, so
as not to hold up the writers and operations behind them: a page sweep holding one is
asked to let go, an autovacuum worker asked, once, to cancel its task (as
Postgres cancels one that blocks a lock waiter) and waited for, until
deadlock_timeout after the move first asked one, and any other holder
waited for, tried again a little later each time, until
prolly.restore_lock_wait (100 ms) after the move first waited: a
statement lets go within a moment, a transaction left open won't soon,
and the rows written instead.  lock_timeout, if set, caps both; the
deadlock detector doesn't see these waits, so they are bounded, for the
whole move.  A restore or share given up so is counted in
pg_stat_prolly's restores_lock_busy.  Nor is a table
restored whose baseline
holds the commit's rows with fewer than prolly.restore_min_changes (2) of
them changed since, as its change finder tells from no more than that many
of its records (the log's entries, none read as a fold reads them):
restoring a table costs about as much as writing a few rows back and
syncing their pages as the commit's are kept again, so a reset of a row
writes it.

A restore is MVCC-safe where TRUNCATE isn't: the storage it replaces
isn't dropped at commit but kept, a retired entry of the branch's kept
list naming the storage itself (its relations' snapshot
BRANCH_KEPT_STORAGE), and shared memory names each relation's new
storage's predecessor and the move that replaced it
(branch_note_replaced()).  A transaction whose snapshot can't move and
doesn't see the move reads the old storage: the relation cache gives its
entry the old storage as it finds the relation's (RelationInitPhysicalAddr()
asks branch_storage_hook), until the transaction ends, so it reads the
rows it saw, its indexes and TOAST table alike.  It can't change them: a
write, a row lock or a definition change of such an entry fails with a
serialization failure, as a REPEATABLE READ write of a row changed since
its snapshot does.  Statements of other transactions hold the
relation's locks while they read it, so the move waits for them.  The
first keep after every snapshot older than the move has ended (the
oldest xmin any backend holds is later than the move) lets go of the
old storage, dropping it as its transaction commits; a crash leaves it
to the first keep after the restart, and a branch's deletion drops it.
A snapshot imported into a transaction (or a parallel worker's, restored)
may be older than a move after which its backend built the relation's
entry: as it is set, every such entry is found again
(RelationCacheRecheckReplaced()).  Shared memory names at most 1024 such
storages, a restore reserving its relations' entries before it replaces
anything (branch_replaced_reserve()): with none free (that many still
needed by older snapshots), the table isn't restored and its rows are
written, MVCC-safe; an entry is never let go of while a snapshot may
need it.  Only the primary's snapshots are covered: the registry is the
primary's shared memory, so a hot standby's query whose snapshot is older
than a reset it replays reads the restored pages, as after a TRUNCATE
(which its replay also waits for queries holding the table's lock
for), and the old storage is let go of by the primary's horizon, which
covers a standby's snapshots only with hot_standby_feedback.  A share of
another branch's storage or kept pages isn't kept so either: a snapshot
older than it reads the shared pages, as after a TRUNCATE.

A branch's first use at a commit another branch keeps the pages of is
made of those pages (branch operations' make_kept()): each such table's
relations share the snapshots, O(1), and the source's writers are not
locked out (the source's relations are held with ShareUpdateExclusiveLock,
tried and never queued for, which only definition changes, VACUUM and
letting go of kept pages wait for); only relations not kept (a table made
or whose storage was replaced since, a sequence) are copied as before,
under locks against writers.  The new branch's baseline takes those
tables' entries from the kept baseline.  A commit that stops being kept is
retired rather than let go of, and its pages are let go of by a later keep
once the retiring transaction has committed.

A restore gives each table it restores new storage, and the storage it
replaces, where every other kept commit's snapshot of the table was, goes
with it at commit; so does a rewrite's, or a dropped table's.  Each keep
and each restore asks what is left of each kept commit's pages (branch
operations' kept_status(); pg_stat_prolly's kept_status_checks counts the
relations asked of).  Only the commits still kept are asked, never more
than prolly.kept_commits and the one a restore keeps again: a retired
commit is neither counted nor offered whatever is left of its pages, and
the retired pile up within a transaction (each of its commits retires
one) until it ends, so asking them too would make each commit of a
transaction ask of every one before it.  So a retired commit whose
storage goes later stays in the record, keeping its commit and baseline
from collection, until a later keep lets go of it or the branch is
deleted.  One with nothing left at all (every
relation's storage, or its snapshot there, gone, as the transaction sees
it) is dropped from the list at once, with nothing to let go of, and its
commit and baseline are no longer kept from collection.  One none of
whose tables can be given its pages any more, but with a snapshot still
left (a TRUNCATE gives a table and its indexes new storage, but not its
change log; its rows' transaction ids are older than the database still
knows), is retired, as above: it stays in the record, keeping its commit
and baseline from collection, until a later keep lets go of its
snapshots.  Neither counts toward prolly.kept_commits or is offered to a
move or a first use.  A commit with a table whose relations are all still
on the storage that kept them stays kept (restore_pages() may still pass
the table over, renamed or with other relations since).  Snapshots that a
dropped commit shares with one let go of are kept until a later keep: the
change that made it gone may roll back.

Kept commits are kept states of the materialization cache
(prolly_matcache.h), beside kept checkouts: the cache's visit() gives
the kept commits its filter asks for, of a commit or holding a data tree
(the refs' kept_holders(), then kept_commits(), each recording the
transaction that kept it, which a snapshot must see committed: refs
format 9) with their commits' roots, and every user of kept states takes both kinds.  A
first use at a kept commit is made of its pages, found through the cache.
A move of any branch (a reset, a checkout's move, a first use's) gives a
table whose rows differ from the target's, where a kept commit of any
branch holds the target's table and sharing costs less than writing the
rows, and the transaction that kept it precedes every snapshot in the
cluster, storage made of that commit's snapshots (branch operations'
share_kept(): O(1) each, the source branch held against deletion and its
relations against letting go of the pages, each relation stored alike on
both and the source's still on the storage that kept it), its baseline
entry taken from the kept baseline; and the move planner costs a first
use's candidates with those tables shared, as it does a kept checkout's.
So the old heads a branch has moved on from serve other branches until
they retire.  (The pages are the source branch's: a snapshot older than
their keeping could see versions it made and deleted before the commit,
rows in no commit; while one is open the rows are written instead.)

Kept materializations (prolly.kept_checkouts, 4 by default, per database)
make a later checkout of the commit a branch switch, its dependencies
checked as a first use checks them.  At a first use, the move planner
takes a kept one as any other branch, by its root, and costs every
candidate's copies with the tables a kept one holds as the target does
shared rather than written (a copy the planner empties to reload is
shared into too), so the copies chosen are those cheapest to finish.  A
checkout's sweep lets go of those
past the budget, the least recently checked out first (recency is kept in
shared memory; after a restart, older branches go first).  The budget
counts idle ones: one a session is on is neither counted nor let go of,
nor is the one a detached checkout's sweep is on.  One another sweep let
go of meanwhile counts as gone, so two sweeps at once let go of no more
than one would.  Letting go of one is deleting its branch, which
releases its relations' maps.  Writer branches done with are swept as
before, apart from the budget.  When something depends on a role being
dropped, DROP ROLE lets go of its database's idle kept checkouts and
checks again; another database's are let go of only from that database
(the error then has a hint).

With dolt.transaction_commit on, a transaction that changed a branch makes
its commit as it commits: the refs, opened by its first write, call
prolly_refs_end_hook at their pre-commit, before the chunk store's own, and
the commit is made there (none if nothing changed since the head).  It is
the transaction's own row changes on the head as it is then: heap reports
the TIDs of the tuple versions each write made or replaced
(branch_tuple_hook), and the change finder applies those the transaction
made and didn't replace, or replaced and hadn't made, to the head's table
trees (fold_rows), with no fold and no snapshot checked.  A transaction
that changed definitions, or more rows than it keeps (or than
maintenance_work_mem holds), or whose rows can't be applied so (a table
the head describes otherwise), commits its working set instead.
Such a commit descends the same trees many times (the refs' root for each
entry it gets and puts, the head's commit and root, each row's path for its
seek and then its apply), so it opens a tree keep_nodes() scope: each node
above the leaves, and each root, is read from the chunk store once (up to
maintenance_work_mem of them), and a
3-row commit reads about its rows' paths.  An apply keeps them so on its
own too, as each level's pass starts from the root.

A fold isn't made again while nothing it reads has changed: the refs
(refs_generations.c) keep in shared memory each branch's generation,
moved as a transaction that wrote the branch ends (every writer is told of
through branch.c), an epoch moved by every catalog change sent and every
collection, and the root the last fold of the branch made, known once its
transaction commits.  Reading the working set (status, diff, a branch's
dirtiness) takes that root while the generation and epoch stay and the
reader's snapshot sees every writer that ended.
debug_prolly_verify_fold_caches folds again and compares.

The fold worker (prolly_folder.c, prolly_fold_worker) takes a working-set
checkpoint of each branch written, in the background, so a status or a
commit folds only what came since, by default at every pass.  With
prolly_fold_worker_batch_rows set, a branch whose writes go on is folded
once its writers since its last fold wrote prolly_fold_worker_batch_rows
rows (the refs count them, each row a write changes) or the first of them
waited prolly_fold_worker_max_delay; one whose writes stopped (nobody
writing it, and no writer ended for a naptime) at the next pass, as is one
no writer of which ended since (nothing to batch). A fold writes the nodes
on the paths to the rows it changes, so folds of a few rows each write the
upper nodes again: batching writes keeps a busy branch's chunk writes, WAL
and journal near its rows' own, while a commit or status during the writes
folds the batch not yet folded itself (so batching is off by default).
pg_stat_prolly counts the worker's folds (fold_worker_folds), the
branches it left for a later pass (fold_worker_deferred), and its
attempts rolled back, the refs held, the branch moved meanwhile, or a
relation's lock held, to be made again (fold_worker_retries), each
branch's tries backed off (after
1, 3, 7... passes, 63 at most).  The launcher takes the databases written
in turn, and a worker exits after 64 passes, so a database written
without pause doesn't keep a slot from the others.  The worker's fold
waits for no relation's lock (checkpoint_unless_moved() sets lmgr.c's
RelationLocksNoWait through it; a lock not free at once rolls the fold
back, to be made again): it keeps its locks on the change logs it
consumed past its lock on their tables, so a DROP of several tables,
holding them and waiting for one's log, would deadlock with a fold
waiting for the next table.  The worker's transactions are read
committed, whatever default_transaction_isolation says, as autovacuum's
are, and predicate locking passes them over
(PredicateLockingPassOverBackend()): a fold writes for as long as it
reads, and its xid, running all the while, would otherwise hold down the
xmin of every serializable transaction begun meanwhile, keeping each one
that commits, its predicate locks and rw-conflicts with it, until the fold
ends; under a busy serializable workload the rw-conflict pool runs out.
The launcher, and autovacuum's launcher and workers (whose automatic
folds, collections and sweeps write in long transactions too), are passed
over the same way; their transactions are read committed already.
Autovacuum's
automatic fold (prolly.autofold_threshold), the backstop for the
database's default branch, folds the same way, before taking the refs,
and gives up while another holds them: a commit never waits for it.

The fold worker's folds can be paced as vacuum is, under the cost-based
delay: prolly_fold_worker_cost_delay (ms; 0, the default, is off) and
prolly_fold_worker_cost_limit (-1 takes vacuum_cost_limit).  While a fold
of the worker's is paced, the buffer manager charges the pages it reads
and dirties (the change logs', the tables', their indexes'), and
prolly_fold_delay_point(), in the folds' row loops (the log finder's and
the full fold's), charges the tree nodes read since as hit pages and
those written as dirtied ones (each is appended, WAL-logged and synced),
then naps once the limit is spent; pg_stat_prolly's
fold_worker_cost_delays counts the naps.  So background folds take a
share of the I/O and CPU, not all they can.  A paced fold holds its locks and its
snapshot through its naps, so it gives way to whoever waits for them:
before each nap, if another backend waits for a lock that conflicts with
one it holds, fast-path ones included, or for its virtual transaction (as
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY waits for older snapshots) (AnyLockHasWaiters()),
it gives up, a retry
(fold_worker_retries), the branch backed off; and it naps 10 s in all at
most, then goes on unpaced, so its snapshot holds the xmin horizon back
no longer.  A reload of the settings reaches a fold under way.  The trade: a paced fold
takes longer, so more writes queue behind it, which batches them (fewer
chunk writes) but leaves a commit or status during the writes more to
fold itself.  It is off by default; fold_worker_retries showing folds
lost to commits, under load, is the reason to turn it on.  No other fold
(a commit's, a status's, autovacuum's automatic fold) is paced.

A rewrite of a versioned table (ALTER TABLE changing a column's type,
REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW) reads and writes every row: as it ends, the
branch is folded as a checkpoint (prolly.fold_on_rewrite, prolly_vcs.c's
prolly_fold_rewritten(), called by tablecmds.c and matview.c), so the
commit after, the fold worker off or in the same transaction, folds only
what changed since.  The checkpoint never waits for a lock (the rewrite
holds its table exclusively, and a commit holding the branch may be about
to read it): a lock not free at once skips it, leaving the table's next
fold to read it in full.  So does any other error the fold meets (it
folds every table of the branch, and one may hold what a commit refuses),
logged with the error's fields; a cancel, or an internal error (class
XX), fails the rewrite instead.  pg_stat_prolly's
rewrite_checkpoints_skipped counts the checkpoints skipped.  dolt_pending_full_folds() lists the tables whose next
fold would read them in full, and why, from the baseline, the catalogs and
each change finder's full_reason(), reading no rows.

A move of the session's branch (a reset, a fast-forward, a merge's)
folds its working set, if not known, as a checkpoint: the fold is the
branch's baseline.  After the rows are written, the baseline is advanced
to the target without another fold, each table the move wrote holding
the target's rows and its change log's record of the move's own writes
consumed (the canonicalizer's advance_baseline()); where it can't be (the
definitions moved too, a materialized view's rows did, a table's state
can't be advanced), the rest is folded as before.  So a move from a dirty
working set folds it once.

That fold is only compared with: once the baseline is advanced, it names
the target's trees, not the fold's.  So the fold, and every tree the move
builds on it, is kept in the backend's memory, not put in the chunk store
(chunkstore_local.c, refs_copies.c's settle_compared()).  Between
prolly_local_chunks_begin() and _end(), the canonicalizer's environments
put their chunks in a table in the backend's memory, and every
environment of the server's trees (prolly_folded_tree_env()'s) gets them
from there; their has() and put() stay the store's, so a durable tree
never takes a chunk only the backend has for stored.  The baseline is
kept beside the refs meanwhile (the refs' chunks are the store's), the
fold isn't published to the fold caches, and the value memo remembers
nothing from it.  At the end, prolly_local_chunks_persist() puts those of
the backend's chunks the baseline reaches, descending only into chunks
the store hasn't: its own few nodes when it was advanced, the fold's
trees it still names where it couldn't be (as before).  So a hard reset
of 1,000 dirty rows over 100,000 appends 4 chunks, not about 1,250.  The
table is bounded by maintenance_work_mem: past it, what it keeps is
persisted and the rest of the step puts in the store as before (a fold of
most of a table, which the baseline mostly can't be advanced past
anyway).  A tree only the backend keeps may still take a stored value's
tree by its TOAST pointer, or the memo's reference to one: each is stored,
which is where persisting stops.

What a fold works out for every table, changed or not, is cached in the
backend (canon_folded.c's own): each table's description and the hash its
baseline is kept by, dropped by a relcache invalidation of the table or a
syscache one of anything a description names; the tree of what versioned
objects depend on, against an epoch every such callback moves (inval.c
sends a relcache invalidation of pg_depend or pg_extension themselves for
their changes, which have no syscache); which objects are extensions'
members; and the last canonical tree's items, so the next is applied as
the items that differ (the baseline's too, from the baseline folded from).
debug_prolly_verify_fold_caches works each one taken out again too.

A fold after another in the same backend works out again only what may
have changed (fold_incremental): with no catalog change or collection
since (the refs' mark, moved by every catalog change sent, and the
backend's own catalog epoch), the same deparsing settings, and a snapshot
that sees every writer of the branch that ended, it takes the last
canonical tree's items and redoes those of the tables whose rows were
written since, and every sequence's (their values change outside
transactions), reading no catalog through.  The refs learn which tables:
every row a writer changes names its relation (note_relation()), and as
the writer ends the branch keeps them in shared memory, the last few with
the generation they ended at (refs_generations.c).  A writer of too many
relations, an eviction, a prepared transaction's end or anything else the
mark doesn't cover folds everything.  A commit's fold begins the next
baseline from the one this backend built last, only the written tables'
entries changed.  debug_prolly_verify_fold_caches then folds everything
else again (its work not counted) and compares.  dolt_status reads definitions
without rows and asks each table's change finder (from that table's
baseline entry alone), so a status in full visits every table.  A
materialized view's rows read so are its baseline's: a plain REFRESH
folds the branch as it ends, but a concurrent one changes the rows in
place, so a status folds unless each prolly materialized view's change
finder finds no change since (a keyless table's, only when its log has no
entry since).  The definitions are those the last read here made while
nothing they're of
changed (no catalog change in this backend's epoch, the same deparsing
settings and baseline, the tree still stored), only every sequence's item
worked out again (pg_stat_prolly's definitions_reused).  They're kept
apart from the folds' items, so a status between two commits leaves the
second's fold to work out only what was written.
A later status takes the last one's answer too, when it compared the same
two roots (the version's and the definitions', which holds every table's
description and baseline rows) and the refs know which tables were written
since a mark it took before asking any change finder (written_since()):
only those tables are compared again (pg_stat_prolly's status_reused), so
a status after a row write costs about the tables written, not every
table.  The answer is this backend's own, and is kept only from a
comparison of every table's changes read so: a table whose change finder
can't tell its changes that way (a partitioned one, or a keyless one with
log entries since its baseline) makes every status a fold, and
status_reused never moves.  A materialized view written since the mark
(refreshed concurrently) is asked again as the full comparison asks it.
debug_prolly_verify_fold_caches reads them all again and compares.
A status in any backend takes the last fold of the branch known first,
once writers have ended since (the refs' last_fold(); while it is still
known, nobody writing, it is the working set, and a status whose version
has that root compares no table at all).  The branch's shared entry keeps
that fold's root with the generation it was known at, so the working set
is that root but for the relations written since (written_since() of that
mark), while the baseline is the one it folded from, nothing it left out,
and no catalog change or collection since.  Its sequences' values are read
again (the canonicalizer's sequences_now()), and the version's root and it
are diffed (objects_diffed()): the tables whose items differ are compared
as the known working set's are, rows and all, those written since as the
definitions read without rows compare them (their change finders), and
the other objects as objects() would visit them.  So a status right after
a commit, or a backend's first one, costs the tables written since and
what the diff holds, describes nothing and reads no catalog through
(pg_stat_prolly's status_from_fold).  A type or materialized view the diff
holds, a materialized view written, or a table's rows that can't be told
so leave it to the comparison above.


History horizon
---------------

History is severed, never rewritten, to keep only a window of it here
("archive", window_archive.c).  The oldest commits kept (the boundary) still
name their parents, so no commit's hash changes; the horizon, entries of
the refs (their side entries, space 'h'), records each boundary edge (a
boundary commit and the parent it no longer keeps) and each fragment: the
commits beyond the edges, down to an earlier fragment's, with its tops,
the commits outside it its commits name (its stops), its identity (the
root of a tree of its commits keyed by date then id, the same wherever the
same commits are severed) and a digest over the sorted hashes of the
chunks its commits reach.

What is kept: every commit a ref names (a branch's head, a tag's, a
remote-tracking branch's, a stash's, a merge's or rebase's in progress, a
pin's, a commit a branch's pages are kept as of), each branch head's
commits dated at or after the cut, and every commit on the way from a
branch head to its merge base with each other one, so merging two live
branches never needs a severed commit.  dolt_history_drop(before) severs
what is older and lets it go (a fragment deleted); prolly_history_plan()
says what a sever would do, and prolly_history_horizon() lists the
fragments.  A sever is the database owner's, holds every ref, and takes
the horizon's lock exclusively.

A full collection reads the horizon as the root at its seal has it,
through its own reads (the refs' side_scan_at()), and doesn't follow a
boundary commit's chunks to the parents the horizon names: what only the
fragment reached is freed.  A default collection follows them (as its
settled files keep what they reach), and none runs in the transaction that
changed the horizon, which might yet roll back.  A collection holds the
horizon's lock shared, so no sever or repair moves the horizon meanwhile.

The window is the database's.  prolly.history_warm (how long each
branch's history is kept here), prolly.history_min_commits (how many of a
branch's newest commits are kept however old), prolly.history_cold_batch
(a cut rounded down to whole hours or days, so fragments are whole ones),
prolly.history_max_size, prolly.history_window_action (archive, delete or
none) and prolly.history_archive (a remote's URL, not a name) are read as the
database has them (ALTER DATABASE ... SET: its owner's or a
superuser's), never as a session has them, so no session severs more or
sends history elsewhere by a SET of its own.  A branch's lineage may keep
its own warm, min_commits or cold_batch (dolt_history_window(), the
owner's, kept in the refs' settings); prolly_history_policy() shows what
a branch keeps.  With no window, everything is kept.  A warm window is
never shorter than prolly.history_hot (the page roots' window, as the
database has it): a shorter one is taken as hot, with a notice.  What a
branch's history keeps is the branch's window's: another ref's commit on
it (a commit the branch's pages are kept as of, a reader's pin, a
remote-tracking branch) has no window of its own, though a collection
keeps what the ref names until the ref lets go of it, and no path to it
is kept as for a merge (it is its own merge base).  A tag keeps its own
commit and the database's min_commits; a ref's commit on no branch's
history keeps itself, and the paths to its merge bases with the
branches.

dolt_history_sever() severs what is past the window, as the action says.
It plans, and archives, holding no ref and not the horizon's lock (only
a lock severs and repairs take turns by); the refs moved meanwhile, it
plans and archives again, a few times, still holding none.  Then it holds
the horizon and the refs only to check the plan (planned again if the
refs moved since, which must give the fragment archived) and record it.
Archived, the fragment's tops are sent to the archive (the transfer
sends what it lacks; a walk past the horizon goes on where the remote has
what is severed already, so fragments archive beside one another), the
tops are made heads of the archive's, named by the database (the
cluster's system identifier and its OID) and the fragment's identity, so
the same fragment is named the same however often it is archived, then
every chunk the fragment made is read back from the archive and re-hashed
(an archive that says it has one it doesn't is found out), and only then
is the horizon moved, the fragment's location the archive's URL; with no
archive, nothing is severed and a warning says so.  The archive is opened
as the role severing (the owner), so its checks are the owner's, and the
archive's own permissions to push protect its history/... heads after:
whoever may move heads there may let the history archived go.  A replan
that changed the fragment leaves the earlier one's heads in the archive,
pinning its chunks there; harmless, but they add up.  The archive is given
prolly.history_archive_timeout (ten minutes where unset) to take the
history and give it back; past it the sever is given up, nothing
severed.  Deleted, the horizon records the fragment
and nothing is sent.  prolly.history_max_size isn't enforced yet.

dolt_history_repair(source, fragments) reattaches fragments, newest
first (all, or the newest few), from a remote or clone that has their
history (source, by name or URL) or, archived, from where they were
archived: the transfer receives the tops and what they reach that the
store lacks, re-hashing each chunk, and the fragment's commits, from its
tops to its stops, must give its identity and digest again before its
edges and record are let go of; otherwise nothing changes.  A repair is
the database owner's and holds the horizon's lock exclusively.  The
history then reads as it did: the same commits, hashes, roots, rows and
diffs.

Listings stop at the horizon, as a shallow clone's do: dolt_log, the
history tables and history mounts list the commits kept, with a notice
once a transaction; history walks (a merge base's, HEAD~n) don't go past
it.  Reading a severed commit once it is freed (dolt_at, a diff, a merge
whose base is past it) is an error saying it is past the horizon and how
to repair it.

Remotes
-------

Transfer ("chunks", transfer_chunks.c) moves the chunks a commit reaches
that the other side lacks, re-hashing each chunk it receives.  A remote
("file", remote_file.c) is a directory of table files and a manifest.
What a transfer has seen (prolly_seen.c) is a map held to its share of
maintenance_work_mem: past it, sorted runs in temporary files, merged
four of a level into one, each with a Bloom filter folded smaller as the
map grows, so its memory stays within budget and a lookup checks a number
of runs logarithmic in its size.

Tags are objects of their own, as commits are: a small tree naming the
commit, its tagger, date and message, whose hash is the tag's id, so a tag
is the same in every database.  The refs name each tag's object and keep
its commit beside it, so resolving a tag reads no object.  A push of a tag
sends its object and what that reaches, then moves the remote's tag; a
fetch follows the remote's tags of commits it has (as Dolt's does), and a
clone takes every tag.  A file remote's manifest lists tags on lines of
their own.

The refs tree records its format, and a file remote's manifest its
version; while the prototype iterates, neither is kept readable across a
change: refs or a manifest of another format are refused, never read.


SQL
---

prolly_jsonb.c is the prolly_jsonb_* functions, on JSON trees (Trees and
chunks, above), and the path reads and edits jsonb's operators take on
them (prolly_jsonb.h).

prolly_dolt.c is the dolt_* functions and system views, with Doltgres's
arguments and results, so scripts written for Doltgres run here.  A tag
(dolt_tag, the dolt_tags view) is a revision wherever one is read, after a
branch of its name; checking one out, as checking out a commit's hash, is a
read-only detached session at its commit.

dolt_rebase replays a branch's commits onto another, as Dolt's: on a
branch of its own, dolt_rebase_<branch>, made at the commit replayed onto,
whose rebase state (the plan, the branch rebased and its head then) the
refs keep with that branch.  Each step is a cherry-pick; a reword, squash
or fixup then replaces the pick's commit with one of its working set, and
a commit that became empty is dropped (unless --empty=keep).  The plan is
edited through the dolt_rebase view, whose INSTEAD OF trigger changes the
state.  A step that conflicts stops the rebase as a pick in progress, in
a transaction block or with dolt.allow_commit_conflicts, and --continue
commits its resolution.  Where Dolt aborts the rebase (conflicts the
session can't keep, or a pick whose definitions conflict: a schema
conflict, which the merge's error marks with errschemaconflict()),
--continue runs its steps in one subtransaction, rolls it back and
aborts the rebase, returning status 1 with Dolt's message, since an
error would roll the abort back with the statement; any other error
fails the statement, the rebase left as it was.  A rebase the statement
began itself is rolled back by an error instead.  Conflict stops return
status 1 where Dolt errors, for the same reason.  An abort, or the end
of a rebase, that is rolled back puts the session back on the rebase's
branch, whose deletion is undone (branch_return_on_abort()); a plain
checkout stays the session's, as Dolt's does.  The last
step done, the rebased branch is moved to the rebase's head (refused if
it moved meanwhile), and the rebase's branch deleted.  An edit's stop
may amend the commit it stopped at: dolt_commit('--amend') replaces the
head commit with one of the working set and the head's parents, its
message kept unless -m gives one, as Dolt's, refused with constraint
violations unless forced as a commit is.  An amend and a rebase's steps
replace commits from the head they read, so they hold the branch first,
and fail (40001, to retry) if another's commit moved it meanwhile; the
commit amended away has its kept pages let go of, the commit before it
staying kept.
prolly_vcs.c adds prolly_root_hash() and prolly_checkpoint().

Snapshot mounts (prolly_snapshot.c) are history read through the
planner: prolly_mount(ref, schema, target) resolves the ref to one commit
and makes, in the ordinary schema target, a foreign table (foreign data
wrapper prolly_snapshot, server prolly_local, made at the first mount)
for each table of schema the commit has, defined as the commit describes
it: columns, types and collations, no constraints.  Mounting reads the
commit's root items alone (the canonicalizer's table_metadata()): no row
is read, re-sorted or written.  It refuses what can't be read as stored:
a key ordered by a type, operator class or collation that isn't built in,
a collation whose version changed, an extension ordering it that changed.
The refs pin the commit under the mount's name ('P', the refs' format
10), so a collection keeps what it reaches; prolly_unmount() and
prolly_refresh_mount() drop the mount's tables first, so they wait for
its scans.  A scan reads only the commit its mount pins, rechecks that
the reader may read the table's history (SELECT on the table of its name,
row security not applying), and streams the data tree in key order:
equalities on the primary key's leading columns and a range on the next
seek and stop the scan (the keying's encode_key_bound()), the key's order
is offered to the planner both ways, and an equality joined to another
relation gives a parameterized path that re-seeks on each rescan.  Only
the columns the query uses are decoded.  The quals are all kept as the
plan's filter too.

Commits don't hold secondary indexes.  A mount made with indexes
(prolly_mount(..., indexes => true)) builds a tree of its own for each
btree index the version describes (not a partial or expression index, nor
one ordered by what isn't built in): an entry per row, keyed by the
index's columns under the version's operator families, collations and
options, then the row's data-tree key, holding the row with only the
index's, INCLUDE and primary key columns (the value encoder's project(),
made of the row's bytes).  The pin keeps those trees too (refs format 11:
a pin's kept trees).  An index scan seeks and orders as the primary key's
does, and reads a row from the data tree only where its entry lacks a
column the query uses.  A refresh brings each tree to the new commit by
the data trees' differences when the rows are keyed and laid out alike,
else builds it again; the first build sorts every row's entry in memory
(maintenance_work_mem).  A plain mount builds none and reads no row.

A history mount (prolly_mount(..., history => true)) is dolt_history_table()
as a foreign table: each table's columns as the mounted commit has them,
then commit_hash, committer and commit_date, with a row for each row at
each commit the mounted one reaches (each commit once, merges' parents
too), read as dolt_history_table(NULL::t) reads it: the commit's table
of the name (a table renamed since isn't followed to its old name, as the
functions don't follow it), its columns by identity (one renamed since by
its name there, one retyped since converted, a value that doesn't convert
NULL with a warning once a scan, one the commit hasn't NULL).  A commit
whose table can't be read here (its key ordered by what isn't built in)
is an error, as a commit missing from the store is.  A reader other than
the database's owner or pg_read_all_data is refused a commit whose table
of the name isn't the one here (not made from its relation, walked once a
scan), as dolt_history_table() by name refuses it.  A scan reads a
commit at a time and streams its rows, so LIMIT stops it.  An equality
on commit_hash (a constant, a parameter, or a join's outer row) reads
that commit alone, if the mounted commit reaches it: the history is
walked highest commit first down to that commit's height, and what is
found is kept for the scan's next rescan, so one lookup reads the
commits above it and a join reads each commit once.  The primary key's
bounds seek in each commit whose key is the version's, and a commit keyed
otherwise is read whole.  Planning reads no history (the commits are
estimated by the mounted one's height).  prolly_mounts() shows which
mounts are history mounts.  The dolt_history_table() functions still read
every commit.

Revision-qualified table names (prolly_revision.c) read a table as
another revision has it, as Doltgres's revision databases do:
"db/ref".schema.table in any FROM clause, db the current database and
ref a branch, a tag, a commit or ancestry of one, matched as Dolt matches
a revision's name (a branch's or tag's name exactly, else the one that
differs from it only by case; two such are ambiguous).  The parser
(transformTableEntry()) makes the name a range table entry of
prolly_revision_table(ref, schema, table), its columns the revision's,
and the planner scans it with a custom scan of its own, never through
the function.  The call is the parser's alone (marked as SQL syntax): one
written as a call is refused as it is parsed, since its last argument
names the role checked, and a view's definition prints the name itself.  The name of the session's own branch is an ordinary
table name, and a table outside the versioned schemas is the same on
every revision.  Any other use of such a name (a write, DDL, LOCK) is
refused (namespace.c's catalog-name checks).  A used branch's table is
its copy, read through heap's code under the query's snapshot, so one
query reading several branches reads each as of one moment, and a
branch's uncommitted rows are not seen while its committed working set
is.  The copy's relation entry is built from the rows that branch has
(RelationIdGetBranchRelation(): CATALOG_READ_ON_BRANCH() makes catalog
scans see another branch's rows, and catalog cache lookups of
branch-aware catalogs read the catalog and keep nothing meanwhile; it is
suspended while invalidations are processed and while the session's own
relation entries are built, which are always its own branch's), kept
apart from the session's entries by OID and branch, and invalidated with
them; so are its btree indexes', which the scan seeks for equalities and
a range known before it reads (constants, parameters, a correlated
subquery's outer values), all its quals kept as its filter too.  The
scan locks the copy as a session on the branch does, holds the branch
against deletion as such a session does (pin_branch()), and is refused
where checking the branch out would be (the refs' check_copies_read():
a snapshot that can't move and doesn't see the branch's first use, or a
move that gave it its head's kept pages).  It takes the branch's grants
(USAGE on the schema there, SELECT on the copy), and refuses a table
whose row security applies to the reader: the role the plan checks it as, a view's owner unless the view
is security_invoker (setRuleCheckAsUser() sets the call's last argument,
as it sets a relation's checkAsUser), else the user.  A table's OID by its name on a branch, and that a role may read
all of it, are kept per backend, flushed by any change to a relation's,
schema's or role's catalog rows.  A branch with only its head, a tag and
a commit are read from the commit's tree as dolt_at() reads them, the
table here of the name.  Each scan looks the revision up again as it
begins and matches the plan's columns by name, so a view or a kept plan
reads the revision as it is then (a column it reads gone, or retyped,
is an error); a view's definition prints the name without a positional
column list (a column renamed by an alias is renamed by name in a
subquery), so parsed again it reads the columns by name too.  A copy
with inheritance children on the branch is read only with ONLY, the copy
alone; without it, it is refused.


Core hooks
----------

prolly_shmem.c is a shared-memory subsystem (subsystemlist.h) reserving
an area per chunk store, value memo and refs implementation that asks for
one, and CheckPointProlly() runs in CheckPointGuts(), and
ProllyRecoveryConsistent() beside XLogCheckInvalidPages(), as recovery
becomes consistent; the chunk store asks XLogBackupInProgress() (xlog.c)
before it removes a file a backup's redo may copy from.  Every batch of
shared invalidation messages sent calls prolly_forget_folds() (sinval.c).
createdb() calls
ProllyCopyDatabase() (prolly_database.c) to copy the template's chunk
store.  A temporary or unlogged table is never stored by prolly
(prolly_table_am_for_persistence()).


Tests
-----

src/test/modules/test_prolly runs each interface's conformance suite
against every registered implementation, and tests version control from
SQL and TAP:

	make -C src/test/modules/test_prolly check

src/test/prolly runs Postgres's own regression suites, sqllogictest and
Dolt's scenarios against prolly tables.
